// THE ONLY CI EMISSION THIS REPOSITORY HAS.
//
// Authored fresh on 2026-08-15 as re-add #1 after the CI wipe, deliberately NOT by reviving
// gunbc.ci_workflow's job-spec architecture. That module emitted six jobs across a build ->
// regen -> ci -> heal -> fleet pipeline with per-step timeout rows, artifact transfer between
// jobs, concurrency expressions, upstream-red refusal arms and a floor step whose plan resolved
// batches, workers and a coordinator. None of it is re-derived here, because none of it follows
// from the obligation this workflow discharges.
//
// The obligation is exactly one sentence: every test witness in the repository executes, on
// every push and pull request, and the run is red if any of them is. From that, one job:
// check out the tree, install the toolchain, build the one binary that folds the witnesses, run
// it once. There is no second job, so there is nothing to sequence, nothing to hand an artifact
// to, and no `needs` edge to get wrong.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, so that a later reader does not restore it as an oversight:
// heal, the generated-artifact drift gates, the seven effect gates, the fmt gate,
// merge-admission stamping and the falsifier cadence are all real obligations and all currently
// unmet. They return one at a time, each re-derived from its own first principles under its own
// operator agreement. Their absence here is the declared state of the ladder, not a gap in this
// module.
//
// REGEN WAS ABSENT FOR A DIFFERENT REASON THAN THE REST OF THE QUEUE, AND IS NOW BACK
// (corrected 2026-08-19; supersedes the three paragraphs this replaced, which reasoned about a
// still-open integration/v1-cut race between two candidate cuts). Neither candidate the prior
// text weighed is what actually happened: integration/v1-cut never reached main, and the v1 .dag
// compiler authority was never deleted. Instead the REGEN ROOT CUT (#8406, 3b431f34a9e,
// 2026-08-18) deleted the old `regen_stage0` binary and its RegenVerifyGate/SelfHostStalenessGate
// CI gates, and rebuilt required-regen as one fold in the same binary this job already runs:
// `claim_executor --required-regen` and `--required-regen-fixed-point`, backed by
// src/v1/stage0/src/required_regen_host.rs. The fold still calls the live v1 .dag compiler to
// regenerate stage0's Rust sources and compares the result against the committed, frozen
// src/v1/stage0/src/*.rs files -- its subject was never deleted, only its old CI harness.
//
// So the gap this paragraph used to describe as order-dependent is now a plain, ordinary one:
// the fold exists and has never been invoked from CI. The two steps below close it by adding two
// more invocations of the one binary already built for the floor fold, in the same job, after it
// -- no second job, no artifact hand-off, no new `needs` edge, for the same reason the header
// above gives for not reviving gunbc.ci_workflow's six-job architecture.
//
// THE DRIFT GATES DID NOT RIDE ON THE OLD REGEN EITHER, and still don't: regen failing is a
// self-host fixed-point defect (the emitted stage0 sources diverge from what the compiler would
// emit today), which is a materially different fact from the generated-artifact drift gate (which
// diffs THIS repository's checked-in generated files against what THIS commit's .dag sources
// would emit for THEM). The two steps below add a third, unrelated failure surface to the job;
// they do not change what the drift gate covers.

// THE REQUIRED CONTEXT'S NAME, AND AS OF 2026-08-25 IT NAMES THE AGGREGATION JOB RATHER THAN
// THE FLOOR JOB. The distinction is the whole of what keeps the split fail-closed, so it is
// stated here rather than left to be inferred from the job list below.
//
// WHAT WAS WRONG WITH THE FIRST CUT OF THE SPLIT, measured rather than argued (found in review
// of gunbc#9203, and confirmed against the live ruleset rather than against the workflow): the
// repository's `passing CI` ruleset is ACTIVE and names exactly ONE required status check --
// `witnesses`. THIS CLAUSE PREVIOUSLY ALSO READ `has NO bypass actors`, AND THAT IS NOT A FACT
// A SESSION CAN STATE: `bypass_actors` and `current_user_can_bypass` are answered RELATIVE TO
// THE CREDENTIAL ASKING, and this repository's sessions authenticate as an integration rather
// than a user. Read through a user credential the same ruleset reports a `RepositoryRole`
// bypass at `bypass_mode=always`. Both readings are correct about different subjects, and the
// emptier one was taken twice through the same kind of credential and mistaken for
// corroboration. Nothing in this paragraph rests on it -- every measurement here was taken
// against runs and check-runs -- so the clause is removed rather than repaired. A GitHub
// required context is produced by the JOB, not
// by the workflow, so a second job publishes its own check under its own name and that check is
// not required. The first cut therefore moved regen and v2-emission into a `build` job whose
// failure COULD NOT BLOCK A MERGE: the required `witnesses` check would go green over a regen
// drift or a v2 emission break, and the PR would be mergeable. That is fail-open (DESIGN §5) --
// and worse than the serial run it replaced, because the serial run's single job carried every
// phase into the one context that gates.
//
// WHY AN AGGREGATION JOB AND NOT A RULESET EDIT. No wall inside this repository can constrain
// what GitHub admits to main (the same boundary the merge_group note below records). Landing a
// change whose safety depends on someone editing a setting afterwards is a coverage gap with a
// promise attached, and it has a real window: until the context is added, the lane is unguarded.
// An aggregation job needs no setting change and no window -- the context that already gates
// keeps its name and now answers for BOTH lanes.
//
// THAT ARGUMENT SURVIVED A BOUNDARY MOVE AND ITS FIRST CLAUSE DID NOT. This note read `the
// ruleset is not a .dag fact`, and it is one now: gunbc.repo_ruleset declares the desired ruleset,
// observes the live one, reconciles, applies and READS BACK, and it imports the required context's
// name from witness_floor_workflow_job_id below rather than spelling it again -- so renaming this
// job can no longer leave the ruleset naming a context nothing publishes, which is the failure
// that shows up as a check stuck pending rather than red. What did NOT change is the reason the
// aggregation job exists: a converged authority ACTUATES and DETECTS, it does not gate, so it
// still cannot make a required context exist at the moment a merge is attempted. The window
// argument therefore stands unchanged on its own terms.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS, and it is not the parallelism. The two lane jobs still carry no `needs` edge
// on each other and still start together; only the aggregator waits, and it does nothing but
// read two results. Each lane job's log is findable by its lane name, and the aggregator's own
// output names which lane to open.
data witness_floor_workflow_job_id: String = "witnesses"

// THE LANE JOB IDS ARE THEIR OWN DECLARATIONS, AND UN-FUSING THEM FROM THE LANE WORDS IS THE
// WHOLE OF THIS CHANGE.
//
// WHY A UNIQUE NAME IS NEEDED AT ALL. A GitHub required status check names a CONTEXT, and a
// context is the job's name -- which workflow published it is not part of the name. So `build`
// is not a name this repository owns: `gunbc.fleet_converge_workflow` declares a job whose id
// is also exactly `build`, and a rule naming `build` names two jobs in two workflows, answered
// by whichever green arrives. Enforcement cannot be moved onto a lane job until that lane's
// context is unique across every workflow emitted here.
//
// WHY THE NAME IS NOT COMPUTED FROM THE LANE WORD, and this is the modeling content rather
// than a spelling preference. `build_lane_job_id` was DEFINED AS `required_ci_lane_build`, and
// that read as single authority because the two strings coincided. They are two facts with
// different authorities and different consumers: the LANE WORD is a CLI argument this
// workflow passes to `claim_executor --required-lane`, owned by `gunbc.fabric_witness_run` and
// consumed by an exhaustive `RequiredCiPhase::lane` match inside the binary; the JOB ID is a
// GitHub required-context name, owned here and consumed by a repository ruleset that is not a
// `.dag` fact at all. Their coincidence was the accident, not the authority. Deriving the job
// id from the lane word -- including by concatenating a prefix onto it -- re-fuses them
// through a different door and in the dangerous direction: a lane rename, which is an ordinary
// CLI-vocabulary edit, would silently move a REQUIRED CONTEXT NAME and a check would stop being
// required with nothing going red. The rows below are therefore spelled, and what protects
// them is that every reference to a job id in this file is taken FROM these declarations rather
// than typed, so a rename here moves the `needs` list and the `needs.*.result` expressions with
// it.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not move enforcement. `witnesses` remains the aggregation job
// and remains the one required context the ruleset names, produced exactly as before, so the
// gate is unchanged rather than weakened and no window opens. The lane contexts are published
// and ignored until an operator names them in the ruleset -- a repository setting, and not
// something any wall in this repository can constrain. Until that edit lands the aggregator is
// still what gates; after it, the aggregator's role is diagnosis rather than enforcement,
// because a same-run dependent job is SKIPPED when a lane fails or the run is cancelled at run
// level, and a SKIPPED required context does not block a merge (measured: `mergeable=MERGEABLE
// mergeStateStatus=UNSTABLE`), while root jobs carrying no `needs` edge take
// cancelled/success/failure and are never skipped.
data floor_lane_job_id: String = "required-witnesses-floor"

data build_lane_job_id: String = "required-witnesses-build"

// The workflow's own name, hoisted to a declaration because a second consumer now joins on
// it. gunbc.fleet_desired_admission refuses a workflow_run payload whose workflow_name is
// not the required floor's; spelling "witnesses" a second time there would fork the name
// (DESIGN §3) and the fork would fail open, not loud — a renamed workflow would leave the
// admission comparing against a name nothing produces, refusing every candidate forever.
data witness_floor_workflow_name: String = "witnesses"

// CANCELLATION IS A PER-EVENT FACT, AND THE GROUP CARRIES HALF OF IT. Operator ruling
// 2026-08-17: every push to main must finish; only pull-request runs may be superseded.
// The predecessor collapsed both axes into one group keyed on `github.ref` with an
// unconditional `true`, so consecutive pushes to main landed in the single group
// `witness-floor-refs/heads/main` and each new push killed the previous commit's run --
// main's floor coverage was decided by push arrival, which is exactly the empty-observation
// narrow section 5 forbids: a cancelled run reports no verdict, and no verdict was being
// read as nothing to report.
//
// Setting `cancel_in_progress` false for main is NOT sufficient on its own. GitHub cancels
// PENDING runs in a group regardless of that flag -- it only ever protects a run that has
// already started -- so a main run queued behind a busy fleet could still be superseded
// before it ever executed. The group must therefore be unique per main run, which
// `github.run_id` gives, and the flag must additionally be false there so the two
// mechanisms agree rather than one relying on the other.
//
// The cost is that N concurrent main runs may execute where one used to. That is the
// intended trade -- coverage of every landed commit, at fleet occupancy -- and it is the
// reason the dep-info race under `target/` is worth identifying before this is widened
// further.
data witness_floor_concurrency_group: String = "witness-floor-${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.run_id }}"

// SUPERSESSION IS AVAILABLE ONLY WHERE THIS EXPRESSION IS TRUE, AND THAT FACT NOW HAS ONE HOME.
//
// This expression decides, per event, whether GitHub cancels an in-flight run when a newer one
// joins the group. It was previously an inline literal inside the workflow value below, which made
// it unreachable by name: any consumer reasoning about whether a cancellation COULD have been a
// lawful supersession had to restate `pull_request` in its own module, and a second spelling of one
// fact is the DESIGN section 3 nicknaming violation -- with the specific decay that the two can
// drift silently, since nothing relates a policy literal in a classifier to a string in an emitted
// workflow. Hoisting it changes no emitted byte; it only gives the fact a name to be cited by.
//
// The consumer is gunbc.run_disposition, which derives supersession AVAILABILITY from this row
// rather than hardcoding the event name it mentions.
data witness_floor_cancel_in_progress_expression: String = "${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}"

// The event name the expression above tests. It is declared beside the expression, in the module
// that owns the expression, so the pair cannot drift apart -- and a witness asserts that this name
// actually occurs within that expression, which is the only mechanical link available between a
// GitHub expression string and the event vocabulary it names.
data witness_floor_cancel_in_progress_event: String = "pull_request"

// The binaries the fold actually needs, and only those. `claim_executor` is the fold's entry;
// `gunbc` is the CLI the repository's own tooling invokes. The predecessor build job named
// nineteen binaries, most of which this cut deleted; naming a binary that no step runs buys
// nothing and costs a compile.
// `v1_src_dag_parse` was ADDED 2026-08-19 for a step that ran it, and REMOVED 2026-08-20 with
// that step. The consolidation folded the parse sweep into `--required-ci` as an in-process
// phase calling the same `cli_run` walk, so nothing in this job invokes the standalone binary
// any more -- and the rule stated two lines above is exactly the one its row was breaking:
// naming a binary that no step runs buys nothing and costs a compile.
//
// Caught in review of gunbc#8647 rather than by me, and the way I got it wrong is worth the
// line: the consolidation witness I wrote ASSERTED `--bin v1_src_dag_parse` was present, using
// "CI builds the parse bin" as a proxy for "the parse sweep survives". It is a bad proxy --
// survival is established by the composed run EXECUTING the phase (`parse OK 50 file(s)
// parse-clean`, run 32371293567), never by compiling a binary nothing runs -- so the witness
// was pinning the surplus in place as a requirement.
//
// The binary itself stays in the tree: running the parse sweep alone is the cheapest check
// available while editing src/v1, and it is a thin caller of the same function, not a second
// implementation. What it no longer is, is CI's business.
// WHAT "FULL COMPILE" DOES AND DOES NOT MEAN, named here because the 2026-08-25 split puts a
// job called `build` beside a phase called `v2-emission` and the pair invites exactly one wrong
// inference. The v2 emission phase compiles a `.dag` ENTRY TO RUST SOURCE and stops before
// cargo; the emit-compile phase beside it takes that source the rest of the way and cargo-builds
// it, over its own bounded entry roster and never over the corpus; this step cargo-builds
// exactly the two bins named below, because they are the two the jobs run. NEITHER COMPILES THE REST OF THE RUST WORKSPACE. A declared `[[bin]]` outside this
// list is not built by any required phase, so a hand-authored Rust bin can fall behind a
// generated struct and sit on main indefinitely -- which is not hypothetical: measured
// 2026-08-25, `infer_semantics_witness` fails to compile on main with six `E0063`s against
// `TypeEnv`, and nothing in the required run sees it.
//
// WIDENING THIS TO `--bins` IS NOT TAKEN HERE, and the reason is scope rather than cost: it
// changes what the required check COVERS, which is an operator decision, and it would land red
// on a pre-existing defect this change did not cause. It is named rather than absorbed so the
// gap stays countable -- DESIGN's 2026-08-25 row already declares the class, and this is a
// specimen of it escaping by a mode that row does not enumerate (the `.dag` side is clean and
// the drifted artifact is hand-authored Rust).
data witness_floor_required_bins: List<String> = ["claim_executor", "gunbc"]

fn witness_floor_checkout_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Checkout" },
    id: none,
    uses: checkout_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [kv(key: "fetch-depth", value: yaml_int(n: 0))]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// CACHING IS DISABLED BECAUSE IT WAS DELETING OTHER JOBS' FILES MID-BUILD, and that is a
// statement about this fleet rather than about the action.
//
// setup-rust-toolchain defaults `cache` to true, which runs Swatinem/rust-cache, which
// registers a POST step. Two of that step's cleanup functions are the deleters, confirmed
// against its own source rather than inferred:
//
//   cleanBin(oldBins)    removes binaries from ~/.cargo/bin that were not in the baseline
//                        captured when THAT job started.
//   cleanRegistry(..)    removes directories from ~/.cargo/registry/src that do not match
//                        the CURRENT workspace's dependency set.
//
// Both are correct on a GitHub-hosted runner, where the home directory belongs to one job
// and dies with it. This fleet is self-hosted, and there /opt/actions-runner/<slot>/_work is
// per-slot while /home/ghrunner/.cargo is ONE directory shared by every slot on the host. So
// each job's baseline and each job's dependency set are computed as though it owned the home,
// and its post step then deletes against a home that other jobs are actively building in.
//
// The two observed symptoms are exactly the two functions. cleanBin gives
// `host tool unresolved: "cargo"` — cargo was present at 01:24:52 when the build step used it
// for 2m49s and absent later in the SAME job on the SAME PATH (run 32088114155), which no
// PATH misconfiguration can produce. cleanRegistry gives
// `ring-0.17.14/crypto/fipsmodule/ec/ecp_nistz.c: No such file or directory` during a build
// (run 32087371268, srv2-04), whose downstream noise is the
// `could not parse/generate dep info` class that reads like a target/ problem and is not.
//
// The selection effect corroborates it: the witness step is ~45 minutes and the build step ~3,
// so almost every hit lands in the witness step — which is what an overlap-probability
// argument predicts and what a configuration error would not.
//
// WHY DISABLING RATHER THAN SPLITTING THE HOME. A per-slot CARGO_HOME also works and was the
// first candidate, but it multiplies the registry by the slot count on every host to solve a
// problem caused by a cache layer this fleet does not need: ~/.cargo already persists across
// jobs on a self-hosted runner, so restore-and-save buys nothing here and only the cleanup
// half has an effect. Disabling is the smaller change and removes the mechanism outright.
//
// THE COST, STATED. Nothing now prunes ~/.cargo, so the registry grows without bound and
// becomes a disk-pressure question instead of a correctness one. That is the right trade —
// a full disk refuses loudly, a deleted source file mid-compile does not — but it is a real
// obligation and it belongs to whoever owns fleet disk, not to this file.
// THIS STEP ASKS FOR rustfmt EXPLICITLY, and the reason is a measured incident rather than
// tidiness. Without the components key it installs a toolchain and takes whatever the runner's
// resolved default happens to carry -- so the job passes on a runner whose default has rustfmt
// and fails on one whose default does not, with no relationship to the diff under test.
//
// Observed 2026-08-22 on gunbc#8860: `required-ci: FAILED PHASE regen refused: normalize
// candidate wt_b.rs: spawn rustfmt: No such file or directory (os error 2)`, while the witness
// floor in the same run passed 10130/10130 with failed=0. The regen phase writes the emitted
// artifact as a FIXED POINT of the formatter, so it must spawn rustfmt; nothing in this
// workflow had asked for it.
//
// THE OBVIOUS READING OF THAT ERROR IS WRONG AND THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY PAID TO LEARN IT.
// `gunbc.host_toolchain_components` `toolchain_completeness_authority_note` records the same
// error on the same host class 2026-07-25: rustfmt WAS present on the machine, the job
// principal simply had two rustup toolchains and defaulted to the one without it. A COMPONENT
// IS A PROPERTY OF A TOOLCHAIN, NOT OF A MACHINE, so "install rustfmt on that host" converges
// green while the gate keeps failing. The fix belongs in what this workflow ASKS FOR.
//
// `gunbc.fleet_workflow_steps` `ci_setup_rust_step` already passes this same key to this same
// action; this step was the one that did not.
fn witness_floor_toolchain_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Install Rust toolchain" },
    id: none,
    uses: setup_rust_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "components", value: yaml_string(s: "rustfmt")),
        kv(key: "cache", value: yaml_bool(b: false)),
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// One cargo invocation, no retry arm. The predecessor carried a fallback that re-ran the build
// with CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1 and RUSTC_WRAPPER unset when the first attempt failed; that arm made
// an sccache deficit unobservable by construction (DESIGN section 5, the absorbing fallback), so
// it is not re-derived. A build failure is a build failure and stops the line.
fn witness_floor_build_script() -> String {
  repo_self_build_command(bins: witness_floor_required_bins)
}

// TWO INVOCATIONS, BECAUSE THEY BUILD DISJOINT PACKAGE SETS AND NEITHER SUBSUMES THE OTHER.
// The first is every declared bin target of the v1-compiler package. The second is the seven
// sibling packages of the derived stage0 partition, whose roster gunbc.repo_self_build reads
// from the partition authority itself. Before this pair the build lane covered only the first,
// which is how #9689 left main's whole-workspace build red on E0432 in v1-stage0-v1-infer while
// every required context stayed green -- the partition crates are the ONLY consumers that
// compile a stage0 module through a per-crate reexport closure, so a roster short of a module is
// invisible to the monolith and fatal to them.
//
// THE SECOND COMMAND IS THE EXECUTING FORM OF A WALL THAT HAS NO HERMETIC WITNESS. Its refusal
// is rustc's: a module missing from a roster reds E0432 naming the module and the referring
// module, a roster entry naming no file reds E0583 naming the entry. gunbc.repo_self_build
// carries why a joining witness is not available instead, and names the modeled Rust module
// dependency graph as the trigger for climbing off the compiler as oracle.
fn build_lane_build_script() -> String {
  join(
    [repo_self_build_all_bins_command(), repo_self_build_partition_crates_command()],
    "\n"
  )
}

fn witness_floor_build_step(build_script: String) -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Build the witness fold" },
    id: Present { value: "build_witness_fold" },
    run: concat(build_script, "\n"),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// ONE INVOCATION OF OUR OWN BINARY PER JOB. Everything the run decides -- which witnesses
// exist, what identity each has, which frame it needs, and whether the roster is admissible at
// all -- is decided in .dag by v2.workflow.required_floor. This step passes the source roots
// and a LANE WORD, and nothing else: there is no plan entry, no plan function, no batch id, no
// worker role and no selection flag, because the fold has no such concepts to configure.
//
// A LANE IS NOT A SELECTION. It names which JOB owns a phase, not which phases exist or which
// witnesses run; every phase is owned by exactly one lane and both lanes run on every push and
// pull request. Conflating the two would be the reading that turns a scheduling fact into a
// coverage claim, and it is the reason the lane word is the only thing the transport carries.
//
// ONE INVOCATION PER LANE, AND THE ORDER WITHIN A LANE IS THE PROGRAM'S RATHER THAN THE JOB'S
// (operator directive, 2026-08-20: "i would much prefer if it was all handled within the
// witnesses step and within the gunbc binary, not at a github actions job level").
//
// **THAT DIRECTIVE IS NOW PARTLY SUPERSEDED, BY THE SAME OPERATOR, ON 2026-08-25** -- "we can
// add it as a parallel job in github actions - we can do the same for regen now, we have more
// runners". The half that SURVIVES is "within the gunbc binary": the phases still live there,
// each job names a lane rather than a phase list, and no step's precondition reads another
// phase's verdict, so the step-ladder defect the paragraphs below describe cannot return. The
// half that is SUPERSEDED is "not at a github actions job level", and it is superseded by a
// fact about the transport rather than by a change of taste: PARALLELISM IS NOT EXPRESSIBLE IN
// THE BINARY. Two phases running concurrently means two runners, two checkouts and two
// toolchains, and one process on one runner can thread but cannot acquire a second machine.
// What the directive was protecting against -- SEQUENCING and PRECONDITIONS leaking into YAML
// -- is exactly what does not cross the new boundary: the two jobs carry no `needs` edge and
// no condition on each other, which is the whole content of running them in parallel.
//
// So the paragraphs below describe a step ladder that is still gone and a consolidation that
// still holds WITHIN a lane. They are kept rather than rewritten because what they establish
// -- why a YAML step list is the wrong place for a phase order -- is unchanged by the split,
// and deleting them would take the reasoning with them.
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES: four steps -- parse, regen, regen-fixed-point, floor -- each its own
// process, their ORDER a YAML list, and each one's precondition an `if:` naming another
// step's `outcome`. The fixed-point step additionally received pass 1's digest by READING
// THE RECEIPT FILE the regen process had just written, which is the tell that a process
// boundary sat where a function call belonged: `run_required_regen_fixed_point` has taken
// `pass1_digest: Option<String>` all along and CI passed it `None`.
//
// `--required-ci` runs the required phases in one process and reports the complete ledger:
// the phases are independent, so an earlier failure no longer skips a later phase and one
// defect can no longer hide the others. The line still stops -- nonzero exit on any failed
// phase -- and a phase that could not run reports SKIPPED as its own state, never as silence
// and never as a pass.
//
// THE ROSTER IS READ FROM THE RUN, NOT FROM THIS COMMENT. Each lane announces every phase --
// one `phase <name>` line for the phases it owns and one `ROUTED to lane <other>` line for the
// phases it does not -- so one job's log names the whole roster. The paragraph below records the
// 2026-08-21 cut to three; #9035 then added v2-emission, and the 2026-08-25 split partitioned
// the roster into `build` (regen, v2-emission, partition-crates, emit-compile) and `witnesses`
// (parse, floor). A count transcribed here has already gone stale twice. The cut phases that paragraph
// enumerates stay cut -- neither the addition nor the split restored any of them.
//
// THE ROSTER WAS THREE PHASES, NOT FOUR (operator ruling, 2026-08-21): the src/v1 .dag parse
// sweep, the regen first-generation comparison, and the witness floor. The fixed point left
// the required mode with the four other cut phases, and the in-memory digest handoff that
// justified collapsing it went with it -- so the paragraph above describes the step ladder
// this replaced, and the digest sentence is history rather than a live mechanism. The cut
// capabilities keep their standalone entry points
// (`--required-regen-fixed-point`, `--behavioral-receipt-*`); what ended is their enrolment.
//
// THE FLOOR STEP'S COMMAND IS NOT BUILT HERE. It is `gunbc.fabric_witness_run`
// `floor_run_command`, the same ArgvCommand the fabric authorizes, rendered for this transport
// by `extdeps.exec.command` `shell_command_render`. An earlier revision built the invocation a
// SECOND time -- its own binary path, its own --required-floor, its own --source-root spelling,
// folded over the same witness_layer_roots -- and #8576 then pinned the two together with a
// witness asserting they agreed on source roots. That was validation standing where
// construction was available (DESIGN §5): it stayed green while the binary path, the flag
// spelling or the argument ORDER drifted, because agreeing on the roots is not agreeing on the
// command.
//
// WHY THE PATHS ARE RELATIVE AND cd DOES THE WORK. shell_command_render single-quotes every
// word, citing IEEE 1003.1-2017 §2.2.2 -- correct for an argv and fatal for a word carrying a
// shell variable, since '$ROOT/dag' does not expand. So $ROOT is not IN the argv: the script cds
// to the root first and the argv stays genuinely an argv, which is what lets a modeled renderer
// quote it at all. The v1-parse step already establishes `cd "$ROOT"` here. Same binary, same
// flags, same roots, resolved against the same directory.
//
// witness_floor_source_root_flags IS DELETED HERE, and this clause previously said the
// opposite. It read that the helper survives because the two regen scripts below build
// --required-regen and --required-regen-fixed-point as separate commands, so it still had
// real consumers and its dissolution was someone else's. That was true when written and
// #8647 falsified it: the consolidation folded all four phases into one --required-ci
// process, deleting both regen scripts, so at the moment this branch merged main the helper
// had exactly one consumer left -- the floor script -- and rendering that from
// floor_run_command removes it. The deferred dissolution did not need a separate carrier
// after all; it completed as a side effect of two changes meeting. Recorded rather than
// silently dropped, because a paragraph that once justified keeping something is evidence a
// reader will reuse, and the justification expired before the thing did.
fn required_lane_run_script(command: ArgvCommand) -> String {
  concat(
    concat("ROOT=", concat(ci_repo_root_shell(), "\n")),
    concat(
      "cd \"$ROOT\"\n",
      concat(shell_command_render(command: command), "\n")
    )
  )
}

fn witness_floor_run_script() -> String {
  required_lane_run_script(command: witnesses_lane_run_command())
}

fn build_lane_run_script() -> String {
  required_lane_run_script(command: build_lane_run_command())
}

// THE FLOOR RUNS LAST AND THEREFORE DECLARES ITS PRECONDITION TOO (operator directive,
// 2026-08-20: "move regen before witnesses"). Regen costs ~4 minutes and the floor ~30, so
// ordering the cheap total check first turns a drift report from a 35-minute wait into a
// 5-minute one -- the ordinary cheap-refusal-first argument, and the reason the directive is
// right.
//
// But the reorder is not a pure move, and shipping it as one would have re-created the exact
// defect the two regen steps were added to fix, pointing the other way. A step with no `if`
// inherits GitHub's default `success()` -- the conjunction of every EARLIER step -- so once
// regen sits ahead of the floor, an unconditioned floor would silently acquire regen's verdict
// as a precondition. A regen red would then DISARM the whole witness floor, and 9,008 witnesses
// would report `skipped` because a stale stage0 mirror is a materially different fact that has
// nothing to say about whether the corpus's claims hold. That is the empty-observation narrow
// again -- `the floor did not fail` standing in for `the floor was never evaluated` -- and it
// would be strictly worse than the original, because the population silenced is three orders of
// magnitude larger than the one silenced before.
//
// So the floor's real precondition is stated for the same reason regen's is, and it is the same
// one: the binary exists. The three claims -- floor, regen, regen determinism -- are mutually
// independent except where regen's receipt genuinely feeds the fixed-point step, and after this
// change every step in the job declares what it actually needs rather than inheriting its
// position. Order is now free to change again without silently rewiring any step's condition,
// which is the property that made the original ordering a hazard rather than a preference.
//
// MEASURED CORRECTION, run 32323690924. The paragraph above was right about the hazard and wrong
// about the remedy, and CI refused the remedy rather than any reasoning catching it. On that run
// the build succeeded, so `steps.build_witness_fold.outcome == 'success'` evaluated TRUE -- and
// the floor reported `skipped` anyway, behind a regen failure. The reason is that declaring an
// `if` does not, on its own, displace the implicit `success()`: GitHub substitutes the author's
// expression for the default ONLY when that expression contains a status function
// (`success()`, `failure()`, `always()`, `cancelled()`). A bare `steps.*.outcome` comparison is
// not one, so the default is silently CONJOINED rather than replaced, and every condition below
// still read `success() && <declared>`. The declaration was decorative: the floor remained
// wired to regen's verdict, and the 9,008-witness silencing described above happened on the
// first run that exercised it.
//
// So each condition carries `!cancelled()` -- a status function, which is what actually performs
// the substitution -- rather than `always()`, because a cancelled run should stop, and only a
// FAILED earlier step must not propagate. The discriminator worth keeping: a precondition that
// reads correctly is not evidence that it binds. The tell here was a step skipping while its
// own stated condition was true, which is visible only by reading the step's outcome beside the
// condition's operands, never from the workflow text.
// A PREFIX, NOT A GUARD, despite the name: the value ends in `&& ` and is meant to be
// concatenated in front of a condition. Used bare it emits a YAML expression ending in `&&`.
fn witness_step_status_guard() -> String {
  concat(witness_step_status_guard_expression(), " && ")
}

// The status function itself, without the conjunction tail. The job-level condition needs it
// bare; the step-level prefix above needs it with `&& `. One spelling, two shapes -- a second
// literal would be the fork that drifts the moment the guard is reconsidered.
fn witness_step_status_guard_expression() -> String {
  "!cancelled()"
}

fn witness_floor_precondition() -> String {
  concat(witness_step_status_guard(), "steps.build_witness_fold.outcome == 'success'")
}

// AN INSTRUMENT, NOT A CHECK. It records and it never refuses.
//
// `spawn rustfmt: No such file or directory (os error 2)` refused a required run on 2026-08-22
// and the message reports the syscall and NOTHING about the environment that produced it. So the
// failure destroys its own evidence, and every occurrence is exactly as unreadable as the last:
// two sessions produced three mutually incompatible readings from it in one night (a per-host
// fact, a runner lottery, a missing component), and all three were refuted by data neither
// reading had. The rustup toolchain reported `component rustfmt is up to date` in the same job
// that could not spawn it, so a bare PATH lookup found neither a rustup shim nor a toolchain
// binary -- and no run, red or green, records which of those exists.
//
// THIS IS ALSO WHY IT RUNS ON GREEN JOBS. There is currently no baseline: nobody knows what a
// WORKING job's PATH, shim directory or toolchain list looks like either, so a failed run's
// environment could not be compared against anything even if we captured it. A zero is readable
// only beside a nonzero, and today there is neither.
//
// IT IS GUARDED ONLY ON `!cancelled()`, deliberately weaker than witness_floor_precondition,
// which additionally requires the build to have succeeded. A build failure is precisely a case
// where the environment is in question, so gating the recorder on the build would blind it
// exactly when it is wanted.
//
// EVERY PROBE IS TOTAL BY CONSTRUCTION -- each one falls back to a printed marker rather than a
// nonzero exit, so the step cannot fail and needs no `continue-on-error`. That distinction is
// not cosmetic: `continue-on-error` is a step that MAY report a verdict and has been told to be
// ignored, which is the escape-hatch shape DESIGN 5 forbids. A step with no verdict to give is a
// different thing, and writing it that way keeps it unmistakable.
fn witness_toolchain_environment_probe_script() -> String {
  concat(
    "echo \"host=$(hostname) runner=$\{RUNNER_NAME:-unset}\"\n",
    concat(
      "echo \"PATH=$PATH\"\n",
      concat(
        "which -a rustfmt || echo \"which rustfmt: absent from PATH\"\n",
        concat(
          "which -a cargo rustc rustup || echo \"which cargo/rustc/rustup: absent from PATH\"\n",
          concat(
            concat(ci_toolchain_homes_isolated_or_refuse_command, "\n"),
            concat(
            "ls -l \"$CARGO_HOME/bin\" || echo \"cargo bin dir: absent\"\n",
            concat(
              "rustup which rustfmt || echo \"rustup which rustfmt: no answer\"\n",
              concat(
                "rustup toolchain list || echo \"rustup toolchain list: no answer\"\n",
                "rustup default || echo \"rustup default: no answer\"\n"
              )
            )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
}

fn witness_toolchain_environment_probe_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Record the toolchain environment" },
    id: none,
    run: witness_toolchain_environment_probe_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "!cancelled()" },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// THE BUILD LANE'S RUN STEP. Same binary, same mode, same source roots, one word different --
// which is the whole point of the lane being a parameter rather than a second invocation.
//
// THE STEP NAME DOES NOT ENUMERATE THE PHASES, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION RATHER THAN VAGUENESS.
// It read "(regen, v2 emission)" while the lane carried four, because a label listing a roster
// is a TRANSCRIPTION of something the run itself prints -- and it drifts silently every time the
// roster grows, which it has done three times in a week. The cost is not cosmetic: a session
// triaging a red step narrowed to the two phases the label named and could not reach the third,
// which was the one that failed. DESIGN's standing rule is to name the instrument and never
// transcribe its output; the instrument here is the required mode itself, which announces one
// `phase <name>` line per phase the lane owns before any of them runs. Read the roster there.
fn build_lane_run_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Required CI: build lane (phases named by the run, not by this label)" },
    id: none,
    run: build_lane_run_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: witness_floor_precondition() },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn witness_floor_run_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Required CI: witnesses lane (parse, witness floor)" },
    id: none,
    run: witness_floor_run_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: witness_floor_precondition() },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// A DECLARED PROVISIONAL BOUND, NOT A DERIVED ONE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE POINT.
//
// Absent, this job inherits GitHub's 360-minute default -- so it was never unbounded, but
// the bound was nobody's decision and it is longer than any observed run needs. The cost it
// imposes is not wall clock: a self-hosted runner is held for the duration, and the step's
// log is UNREADABLE until the job ends, so an over-long ceiling converts a failed run into
// hours of zero information. A bounded failure is strictly more informative than an
// unbounded wait.
//
// 180 is NOT derived from a completed run, because no run of this fold has ever completed.
// Deriving it would require the completion time this bound exists to force. What it is: half
// the inherited default, above every phase sequence measured so far (preparation 553s plus
// published-mock projection 47s locally, at bfecfd961c1), and low enough that the log
// arrives the same working hour as the failure. Calling it derived would be the fabricated
// plausible output DESIGN section 5 forbids, one level up -- a number with no measurement
// behind it wearing a measurement's authority.
//
// The two regen steps added 2026-08-19 are covered by the SAME job-level bound, provisionally,
// for the same reason: neither has ever completed in CI either, so there is nothing to derive a
// separate figure from yet. This is a widening of what the existing provisional number covers,
// not a second number -- DESIGN §2 (one concept, one authority) applies to a timeout bound as
// much as to any other fact.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER: the first run that completes this fold, floor and regen alike. Real wall
// times replace this constant -- at which point this note dissolves with the provisional bound
// it explains.
// ONE LIST, TWO PROJECTIONS. The steps GitHub sees and the capability roles the closure reads
// are derived from the same rows, so they cannot drift apart. A second hand-maintained list of
// roles beside the step list would be the §3 fork this closure exists to close, reintroduced as
// its own fix.
type WitnessFloorBoundStep {
  step: Step
  role: StepCapabilityRole
  step_name: String
}

// EACH PATH IS ONE ROW BECAUSE TWO CONSUMERS NOW READ IT. The writer learns the filename from
// the environment variable this job sets; the matching upload step names the same file to
// collect. Spelled twice -- six literals across the three files -- each pair is free to drift
// silently in the worst possible direction: the uploader looks for a file the writer never
// wrote, `if-no-files-found: error` fires, and the failure reads as "the floor produced no
// roster" when the truth is "the writer and the uploader disagree about a filename". One row
// per file makes that disagreement unrepresentable rather than caught.
data required_floor_disposition_path: String = "required_floor_disposition.tsv"

data required_floor_disposition_artifact_name: String = "required-floor-disposition"

data expected_red_roster_join_path: String = "expected_red_roster_join.tsv"

data expected_red_roster_join_artifact_name: String = "expected-red-roster-join"

data long_home_storage_agreement_path: String = "long_home_storage_agreement.tsv"

data long_home_storage_agreement_artifact_name: String = "long-home-storage-agreement"

// THE FOURTH FILE, AND THE ONLY ONE THAT WAS NEVER EVEN WRITTEN.
//
// The three rows above record files the floor wrote on every run and nobody collected. This one
// is a rung below that: `cli_run.rs` reads GUNBC_REQUIRED_FLOOR_CLAIM_COST and calls
// `write_required_floor_claim_cost_tsv` -- one row per EXECUTED claim, carrying identity, module,
// outcome, verdict_reached, and BOTH clocks (wall_ms and cpu_ms) -- but this job never set the
// variable, so the writer was never armed and the population was recomputed and discarded on
// every required run since the floor cut.
//
// WHAT THAT COSTS, and it is a measurement the corpus could not take rather than a file it
// could not read. The floor carries TWO INDEPENDENT BLOCKING DEADLINES on two different clocks:
// `v2.workflow.required_floor` `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms` and
// `required_floor_claim_wall_safety_limit_ms` -- NEITHER VALUE IS TRANSCRIBED HERE, because this
// clause carried a parenthesised value beside each name and the wall one was simply wrong -- it
// disagreed with the constant set by the very change that wrote the note, and with the preemption
// fixture one file away. Two numerals, one authority, and the copy rotted inside its own diff.
//
// THE FIRST REPAIR OF THIS CLAUSE RE-TRANSCRIBED THE VALUES WHILE EXPLAINING WHY NOT TO, which is
// why no figure appears above: writing "the wall limit is set to N" to document the rot recreates
// exactly the coupling that produced it, one sentence later. A rot receipt names the constant. The
// constants are named; a reader who needs a value reads the function. They are deliberately
// different from each other so a hermetic
// in-process claim descheduled by a co-tenant host does not read as a runaway. The only
// per-claim two-clock output any run actually emits is the `[over-cost]` log line -- and that
// population is selected by `required_floor_cost_basis`, which is the constant `CpuCost`. So a
// row is printed because its CPU exceeded the diagnostic cost line, and its wall column is
// carried along.
//
// The consequence is that the CPU margin distribution is derivable from run output and the WALL
// margin distribution IS NOT, at any sample size: a claim with low CPU and high wall -- exactly
// the blocked-or-descheduled failure the wall limit exists to catch -- is never printed at all.
// Measured on the five main runs pinned in the PR body (33036922596, 33037011305, 33037689214,
// 33037870402, 33037904520; 690 pooled rows): wall minus CPU is at most 15 ms on every printed
// row, mean 6.7 ms. That is not evidence that no wall-bound claim exists; it is the selection
// effect stating that this sample cannot contain one. Reading a wall distribution off it would
// be a population selected on one clock reported against the other clock's limit.
//
// So this is not a convenience artifact. It is the only route to the wall limit's margin
// population, and arming it changes no threshold and no verdict -- `write_required_floor_claim_cost_tsv`
// is a pure write of rows the fold already produced.
//
// `if-no-files-found: error` IS SAFE HERE ON THE SAME GROUNDS AS THE THREE ABOVE, and checked
// the same way rather than inherited: the write is guarded only on its own path being set,
// which this job now sets, and it is reached on the same fold-completion path as the disposition
// and long-home writes -- strictly EARLIER in that sequence, so any refusal that returns before
// those three also returns before this one. It is never absent on a run that reached its writes.
data required_floor_claim_cost_path: String = "required_floor_claim_cost.tsv"

data required_floor_claim_cost_artifact_name: String = "required-floor-claim-cost"

// THE FLOOR HAS BEEN WRITING THREE FILES ON EVERY RUN AND THROWING ALL THREE AWAY.
//
// cli_run.rs reads GUNBC_REQUIRED_FLOOR_DISPOSITION, GUNBC_EXPECTED_RED_ROSTER_JOIN and
// GUNBC_LONG_HOME_STORAGE_AGREEMENT, and `write_required_floor_disposition_tsv`,
// `write_expected_red_roster_join_tsv` and `write_long_home_storage_agreement_tsv` emit them.
// The disposition file carries one row per OFFERED site: identity, disposition, and the matched
// prefix where one applies. All three environment variables are already set on this job.
// Nothing ever collected any of the files, so every run tore its runner down and took them.
//
// This is ONE unwired last hop with three instances, not three similar things -- so it is fixed
// at the class, by one step shape applied three times. Fixing one site would leave the other two
// for someone to rediscover, paying the whole investigation again to reach a conclusion already
// written down here.
//
// WHAT THAT COST, concretely: gunbc#8860 added three witnesses and could not show they were
// discovered. The `planned=` delta looked like proof and is not -- discovery is deterministic
// per commit but confounded, one unrelated line has been measured moving `offered` by 14 -- and
// the log carries no per-identity output for a passing witness. So the run answered neither
// "did my witness run" nor "was it declined, and why", while holding the file that answers the
// second one.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT ANSWER, and the PR body says it too: a row reading Planned means the
// identity was DISCOVERED AND ROUTED. It does not mean the claim executed, and it certainly
// does not mean it passed -- `ClaimOutcome::Pass` increments a counter and discards the
// identity, so no execution roster exists anywhere to upload. This narrows the question; it
// does not close it.
//
// `if-no-files-found: error` IS SAFE ON ALL THREE, and that was checked rather than assumed --
// `error` on a file the run does not always write would redden green runs. cli_run.rs guards the
// join file on `roster_join_active`, which is `GUNBC_EXPECTED_RED_ROSTER_JOIN.is_some()`, and
// this job sets it; the other two are guarded only on their own paths being set, likewise set
// here. So all three are written whenever the floor reaches its writes. The one way a file is
// absent is a refusal that returns before them -- a run whose line has already stopped, where a
// second red is noise rather than a fabricated verdict.
//
// `if-no-files-found: error` IS ALSO LOAD-BEARING ON ALL THREE, not decoration. The default is `warn`:
// it would upload nothing, go green, and hand a reader an empty artifact in which "no sites
// were declined" and "the file was never written" are indistinguishable. A blank is not
// evidence. `gunbc.fleet_workflow_steps` `ci_release_bins_upload_step` already sets it.
//
// THE CONDITION UPLOADS ALL THREE AFTER A FAILED FLOOR TOO, the case that matters most: a run
// that failed is exactly when someone wants to know what was admitted. It reuses
// witness_floor_precondition -- not cancelled, and the fold was built -- rather than the
// action's success-only default, so a red floor still publishes all three files.
// WHY THREE STEPS AND NOT ONE BUNDLED STEP WITH THREE PATHS, MEASURED RATHER THAN PREFERRED.
// gunbc#8875 landed the bundled form: one artifact, three newline-separated paths,
// `if-no-files-found: warn`. Flipping that one flag to `error` does not produce this step's
// guarantee, and the difference is in the action rather than in taste. actions/upload-artifact
// resolves all path patterns through ONE globber into ONE combined file list, and evaluates
// `if-no-files-found` on `searchResult.filesToUpload.length === 0` -- the whole list, once. So
// under the bundled shape, with EITHER flag value, two files present and one missing uploads
// the two and says nothing: the absence that this evidence exists to make visible is exactly
// the absence the bundled step cannot report. One step per file is what moves that from caught
// to unrepresentable, because each step's list is the one file it names.
//
// THE INCIDENT THAT PAID FOR THIS, carried forward from the bundled step rather than dropped
// with it: a PR was blocked by sixteen failures in witness families its diff could not reach,
// the summary counters said fifteen identities had moved between dispositions, and it could not
// be closed because the rows naming WHICH fifteen no longer existed anywhere. Two independent
// recomputations from the trees then disagreed with the floor's own counts at both ends -- so
// the only instrument that could settle it was the one the job was deleting. A receipt computed
// and dropped is worse than one never computed, because the summary it backs keeps being quoted
// as though the detail were available.
//
// long_home_storage_agreement.tsv is in the set deliberately rather than incidentally: it is
// the row that records a file whose PATH and whose declared MODULE disagree about long-home,
// which is the first hypothesis any recurrence of that incident has to eliminate.
//
// CANCELLATION IS EXCLUDED, and that is a separate fact from the build guard below: a cancelled
// run's TSVs are partial by construction, and a partial receipt read as a complete one is the
// fabricated-output failure.
fn witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(step_name: String, artifact_name: String, path: String) -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: step_name },
    id: none,
    uses: upload_artifact_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "name", value: yaml_string(s: artifact_name)),
        kv(key: "path", value: yaml_string(s: path)),
        kv(key: "if-no-files-found", value: yaml_string(s: "error")),
        kv(key: "retention-days", value: yaml_int(n: 14)),
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: witness_floor_precondition() },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// ONE PREPARATION, RENDERED TWICE. Checkout, toolchain and build are byte-identical in both
// jobs, so they are authored ONCE here and each job appends only the step that distinguishes it.
// Two copies would be two definitions of one fact, free to drift the moment either is edited --
// the §3 fork this file already closes for the step/role pair, reintroduced across jobs.
// THE SAME ACTION PROVIDES THE SAME CAPABILITIES WHEREVER IT RUNS. The Install Rust toolchain
// binding below declared only [Cargo, Rustfmt] while gunbc.fleet_workflow_steps ci_setup_rust_step
// declares [Cargo, Rustc, Rustfmt, Rustup] for the identical setup_rust_action -- one action with
// two capability stories, which is the DESIGN 3 fork. It went unnoticed because nothing here
// consumed Rustup until the pin step did, and then the closure refused. The under-declaration is
// corrected rather than the consumer weakened: the action really does install rustup and rustc.
//
// THE TWO LANES BOOTSTRAP DIFFERENTLY AND THAT DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT, not an oversight.
// The floor lane builds the two bins it actually executes. The BUILD lane builds every declared
// bin (`--bins`), because host-Rust coverage of the whole bin roster is a fact somebody has to
// establish and this is the lane whose subject is "does this repository still build" -- see
// `repo_self_build_all_bins_command`. Duplicating the narrower build in the floor lane would
// buy nothing: the lanes are runtime-independent by construction (no `needs` edge, no artifact
// handoff), so their bootstraps are independent CPU, not a shared prerequisite one could save.
fn prepared_bound_steps(build_script: String) -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  [
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_checkout_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Checkout",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: toolchain_home_isolation_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Isolate toolchain homes",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_toolchain_step(),
      role: provides_only(capabilities: [
        CargoCapability, RustcCapability, RustfmtCapability, RustupCapability,
      ]),
      step_name: "Install Rust toolchain",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: toolchain_pin_rustup_default_step(),
      role: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustupCapability, CargoCapability]),
      step_name: "Pin rustup default (isolated RUSTUP_HOME has no default toolchain)",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_build_step(build_script: build_script),
      role: consumes_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability]),
      step_name: "Build the witness fold",
    },
  ]
}

// THE FLOOR'S RUN STEP WAS DECLARED `capability_neutral` AND THAT DECLARATION WAS FALSE. It runs
// `claim_executor --required-ci`, whose regen phase spawns rustfmt to normalize the emitted
// mirror, so it consumes RustfmtCapability and now says so.
//
// A step that consumes a capability while declaring it consumes none is invisible to the closure
// check BY CONSTRUCTION: the check was honest and had nothing to catch, because the consumer said
// it consumed nothing. That is why the failure surfaced as a runner lottery instead of a refusal.
//
// Cargo is deliberately NOT listed on the run step: it invokes a prebuilt binary and does not
// shell out to cargo. Listing it would trade one false declaration for another.
fn witness_floor_bound_steps() -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  append(
    prepared_bound_steps(build_script: witness_floor_build_script()),
    items: [
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_toolchain_environment_probe_step(),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Record the toolchain environment",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_run_step(),
        role: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustfmtCapability]),
        step_name: "All witnesses (one prepared subject, one fold)",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
          step_name: "Upload the floor's admission roster",
          artifact_name: required_floor_disposition_artifact_name,
          path: required_floor_disposition_path
        ),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the floor's admission roster",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
          step_name: "Upload the expected-red roster join",
          artifact_name: expected_red_roster_join_artifact_name,
          path: expected_red_roster_join_path
        ),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the expected-red roster join",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
          step_name: "Upload the long-home storage agreement",
          artifact_name: long_home_storage_agreement_artifact_name,
          path: long_home_storage_agreement_path
        ),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the long-home storage agreement",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
          step_name: "Upload the floor's per-claim cost receipt",
          artifact_name: required_floor_claim_cost_artifact_name,
          path: required_floor_claim_cost_path
        ),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the floor's per-claim cost receipt",
      },
    ],
  )
}

// EVERY BINARY TARGET COMPILES, AND IT IS A SECOND STANDING RATHER THAN A WIDER BUILD STEP.
// Two populations were being answered by one roster and only one of them was ever authored:
//   RuntimeArtifactPopulation -- the executables this job RUNS: claim_executor and gunbc.
//   BinaryCompilePopulation   -- every bin target v1-compiler DECLARES.
// witness_floor_required_bins is the first and is correct as the first; the rule above it
// ("naming a binary that no step runs buys nothing and costs a compile") is a rule about
// ARTIFACT PRODUCTION and stays intact. What was missing is that nothing answered the second,
// so a bin outside the runtime pair could stop compiling and no required phase would notice.
//
// MEASURED SPECIMEN THAT PRICED THIS STEP: #8952 added `authored_import_names` to TypeEnv and
// did not update `infer_semantics_witness`, whose six synthetic TypeEnv constructions then
// failed E0063. Main stood red at `cargo check -p v1-compiler` and nothing could see it -- the
// Rust suite left CI 2026-07-11, clippy 2026-07-08, the compile-clean gate went with the floor
// cut, and this job builds two of sixteen declared targets. A .dag caller would have refused
// loudly at resolve; a Rust mirror has no such wall once nothing invokes rustc on it, so the
// exposure is SILENT STALENESS rather than a refused closure.
//
// NO NEW ARGV WORD, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. gunbc.repo_self_build is the single authority for
// how this repository builds itself and its note instructs reviewers to refuse a new argv word
// the modeled surface already owns. An empty bin set already renders the no-selector package
// build, which repo_self_build_empty_bins_note records as cargo's own meaning for "build every
// target in the package" -- so the population is DERIVED from the manifest and a new [[bin]]
// joins this standing with no edit here.
//
// BUILD RATHER THAN CHECK, DECIDED BY MEASUREMENT AND NOT BY PREFERENCE. From a cold target dir
// with the two-bin build already paid, the marginal cost of building every target was 12s
// against 52s for `cargo check --release --bins`, and the build additionally LINKS. Cheaper and
// strictly stronger, so there is no tradeoff to split: check would have bought less for more.
// Re-derive with a fresh CARGO_TARGET_DIR, the two-bin build, then this command.
//
// PLACEMENT IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART, AND IT MOVED FROM THE FLOOR LANE TO THE BUILD LANE.
// The original argument was about ORDER WITHIN THE FLOOR JOB and it still holds where it was
// aimed: ahead of the fold this step would be a preparation mask, because an auxiliary target
// failing to compile would stop the job before the floor ran and the planned/executed/terminal
// ledger -- every semantic measurement that job exists to publish -- would vanish behind an
// unrelated compile error. What that argument never asked is WHICH LANE, and the answer it
// silently assumed was wrong twice over.
//
// FIRST, THE FACT WAS ALREADY ESTABLISHED IN THE OTHER LANE. `build_lane_build_script` is
// `repo_self_build_all_bins_command()` -- `--bins`, every declared bin target -- so the build
// lane's PRELUDE already compiles and links the whole binary population before that lane runs
// a single phase. The floor lane's copy was therefore a second authority for one fact (DESIGN
// section 3), and the two never disagreed because they could not: same package, same profile,
// same targets.
//
// SECOND, THE DUPLICATE WAS THE ONE ON THE CRITICAL PATH. The floor lane is the longer of the
// two (64m against the build lane's 41m on green main run 33093831916), so this step's 3m13s
// was pure addition to the required check's wall clock. Moving it to the shorter lane removes
// it from that clock. THAT IS THE WHOLE CLAIM, and it is the only one execution supports.
//
// WHAT THIS STEP DOES NOT DO IS GET CHEAPER, AND AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS COMMENT SAID IT
// WOULD. It predicted "the 12s marginal case the BUILD-RATHER-THAN-CHECK note above measured",
// reasoning that `build_lane_build_script` is `repo_self_build_all_bins_command()` so every
// target this step names is already built when it runs. MEASURED ON RUN 33107459033, THE FIRST
// RUN AFTER THE MOVE: 3m13s, indistinguishable from its cost in the floor lane. The prediction
// was falsified by execution and is corrected here rather than left standing, because a
// transcribed figure that nothing re-derives is exactly the rot DESIGN's name-the-instrument
// rule exists to stop -- and this one was wrong on the day it was written.
//
// WHY THE PREDICTION FAILED, stated as a mechanism this lane's own log supports rather than as
// a certainty: the reasoning looked at the PRELUDE and ignored the 39 minutes between it and
// this step. Run 33107459033's build lane ran `emit-compile` from 19:40:24 to 19:58:54 --
// eight emitted closures assembled and built through cargo -- and `partition-crates compile`
// over 7 packages immediately after, all sharing this job's target directory. By the time this
// step runs, the prelude's warmth is gone. The 12s figure was measured with the two-bin build
// paid and NOTHING ELSE INTERVENING, which is not the position this step occupies in either
// lane. What is CONFIRMED is the duration; the eviction is the mechanism that fits the log and
// is not independently established, so do not cite it as measured.
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE FOR PLACEMENT IS SMALLER THAN IT LOOKS AND STILL FAVOURS THIS LANE. The move
// buys wall clock on the required check, not compute: the same 3m13s is still spent, on a lane
// with roughly 15 minutes of headroom under the floor rather than 23. If the build lane ever
// overtakes the floor lane, this step becomes critical path again and the right response is to
// re-derive both lane durations, not to move it back on the strength of this comment.
//
// WHY MOVED AND NOT DELETED. The two populations above are still two populations, and the
// build lane's prelude answers the second only INCIDENTALLY -- it builds every bin because
// that is a cheap way to produce claim_executor and gunbc, not because anything declares the
// binary population as a standing. Deleting this step would leave that standing resting on a
// prelude's convenience, so a later narrowing of the prelude to the two bins it actually needs
// would remove the coverage with nothing red to say so. The step survives as the named
// standing with its own step id, its own verdict and its own execution provenance; what
// changed is only which job pays for it.
//
// THE ANTI-MASK PROPERTY SURVIVES BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN BY ORDERING. In the floor lane
// it was preserved by putting this step last; in the build lane there is no floor ledger for
// it to mask at all, and the two lanes carry no `needs` edge on each other, so a red here
// cannot stop the floor from publishing its receipts. It keeps `!cancelled()` so a red build
// lane still reaches it.
//
// WHAT IT ESTABLISHES: every declared v1-compiler binary target compiles and links under the
// release profile. WHAT IT DOES NOT: that any of them RUNS correctly, and nothing about test
// targets, which this command does not build.
// THE BUILD LANE'S BOUND STEPS. Preparation is `prepared_bound_steps` -- the SAME rows the
// witnesses job uses -- and this job appends the two that distinguish it: the environment
// recorder (regen spawns rustfmt, so the probe is wanted exactly where that spawn happens) and
// the lane's run step. The binary-population standing that used to close this lane is gone: its
// prelude already builds every bin (`build_lane_build_script`), so the standing was a second
// cargo invocation asking a question the first had answered. No TSV
// uploads: the three receipt files are written by the FLOOR, so a build-lane job naming them
// would demand files this lane never produces and `if-no-files-found: error` would redden it
// for a receipt that was never its to write.
fn build_lane_bound_steps() -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  append(
    prepared_bound_steps(build_script: build_lane_build_script()),
    items: [
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_toolchain_environment_probe_step(),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Record the toolchain environment",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: build_lane_run_step(),
        role: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustfmtCapability]),
        step_name: "Required CI: build lane (phases named by the run, not by this label)",
      },
    ],
  )
}

fn build_lane_steps() -> List<Step> {
  list_map(xs: build_lane_bound_steps(), f: fn(b) { b.step })
}

fn build_lane_step_annotations() -> List<CapabilityAnnotatedStep> {
  list_map(
    xs: build_lane_bound_steps(),
    f: fn(b) { CapabilityAnnotatedStep { step_name: b.step_name, role: b.role } },
  )
}

fn build_lane_capability_closure_holds() -> Bool {
  capability_closure_is_closed(
    v: workflow_job_capability_closure(steps: build_lane_step_annotations()),
  )
}

fn witness_floor_steps() -> List<Step> {
  list_map(xs: witness_floor_bound_steps(), f: fn(b) { b.step })
}

fn witness_floor_step_annotations() -> List<CapabilityAnnotatedStep> {
  list_map(
    xs: witness_floor_bound_steps(),
    f: fn(b) { CapabilityAnnotatedStep { step_name: b.step_name, role: b.role } },
  )
}

fn witness_floor_capability_closure_holds() -> Bool {
  capability_closure_is_closed(
    v: workflow_job_capability_closure(steps: witness_floor_step_annotations()),
  )
}

// THE BEHAVIORAL RECEIPT IS NOT IN THIS JOB AT ALL (operator ruling, 2026-08-21).
//
// It was two steps until #8647 folded it into `--required-ci`, and this paragraph recorded that
// relocation. The ruling then cut it from the required mode entirely, along with
// merge-admission capture and stamping and the regen fixed point, so neither a step here nor a
// phase inside the invocation exists -- and a paragraph explaining where it lives would name a
// place it does not.
//
// WHAT THAT MEANS FOR COVERAGE, kept because the fact outlives the mechanism: no required run
// now compares a changed authority against its committed mirror. The comparison survives as
// `--behavioral-receipt-plan` / `--behavioral-receipt-selftest` / `--behavioral-receipt-census`,
// which have no automated consumer, so it is run deliberately or not at all. That is a declared
// scope narrowing rather than a climb, and naming it here is what keeps its absence from
// reading as coverage.
fn witness_floor_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: floor_lane_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: witness_floor_steps(),
    needs: [],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: not_on_wet_cadence_condition() },
    timeout_minutes: 180,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

// MERGE_GROUP IS ENROLLED SO THE REQUIRED CHECK CAN RUN ON THE COMPOSED COMMIT, NOT ONLY ON EACH
// BRANCH HEAD. Without this trigger a merge queue would create its merge-group commit and this
// workflow would never schedule for it, so the required check would be pending forever -- the
// prerequisite has to exist BEFORE the queue is enabled, not alongside it.
//
// WHY IT IS NEEDED AT ALL, measured rather than argued. Three pairs of PRs have each been
// independently green and jointly broken main: gunbc#9049 with #9114, #9057 with #9062, and
// #8919 with #8992. None involved a textual conflict. In each case the first change altered a
// semantic API and the second was checked against a base where the old API still existed, so both
// greens were true of trees that never existed together. The #9057/#9062 pair took the whole fleet
// red for about an hour on 2026-08-24 and produced four uncoordinated repair PRs plus a fifth
// branch, two of which proposed deleting live code.
//
// THIS ROW IS THE IN-REPOSITORY HALF AND IT IS INERT ON ITS OWN. With no merge queue configured the
// merge_group event never fires, so this changes nothing about how CI runs today; it is a no-op
// until a repository setting is changed, which is deliberate -- the prerequisite lands first so the
// setting can be flipped without a window in which the required check cannot schedule. The other
// half is a repository setting, and as of gunbc.repo_ruleset it is a DECLARED and OBSERVED one
// rather than an invisible one: that module carries strict_required_status_checks_policy false as
// a stated position and refuses on any divergence it can read. It still cannot flip a setting
// nobody has declared -- there is no merge-queue rule in desired, because enabling the queue is an
// operator decision and modelling it as desired would make this module apply a policy nobody
// chose. And no wall inside this repository can constrain what GitHub admits to main in any case.
// Recorded here so the two halves are legible as one change rather than rediscovered.
//
// THE INVARIANT BOTH HALVES SERVE: the commit admitted to main must be the exact composed commit
// that passed the required checks. A merge queue establishes it by construction; requiring branches
// to be up to date approximates it at the cost of a re-run of the ~30-minute floor for every PR
// behind a merge.
// A PROVISIONAL BOUND, DERIVED FROM MEASUREMENTS THAT DO NOT YET INCLUDE THIS JOB.
//
// The two phases this lane owns have been measured on a session worktree, not on a CI runner:
// regen ~9 minutes, and the v2 emission compile of `src/v2/compiler/00_compile.dag` ~7 minutes.
// Serial inside one job that is ~16 minutes, against the witnesses lane's ~30-40. 90 is above
// every measured sequence with room for a slower runner, and low enough that a hung job's log
// arrives the same working hour -- the same reasoning the witnesses job's 180 carries, applied
// to a smaller subject rather than copied from it.
//
// It is NOT derived from a completed CI run of this job, because there has not been one. Calling
// it derived would be the fabricated plausible output DESIGN §5 forbids.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER: the first CI run that completes this lane. A real wall time replaces the
// constant and this note dissolves with it.
//
// NO `needs` EDGE, AND THAT IS THE ENTIRE CHANGE. Two jobs that name each other in `needs` are
// the serial run with extra checkouts; two that do not are the parallel one. The lanes share no
// data -- regen's receipt is consumed by nothing outside its own process, and the floor's three
// TSVs are written and uploaded inside the witnesses job -- so there is nothing an ordering edge
// could be protecting.
// THE RUST UNIT TESTS, IN THEIR OWN JOB AND NOT IN EITHER REQUIRED LANE (operator ruling
// 2026-08-29: "add the rust unit tests but keep them in a separate job for now"). The 774
// `#[test]`s under src/v1/stage0 ran on no CI path before this job existed. It is not a `needs`
// of the aggregate, so it does not gate a merge yet; it is a third context beside the two lanes,
// and promoting it is a one-row edit to `required_lanes_aggregate_job` once its wall clock is
// measured on the fleet runner.
data rust_unit_tests_job_id: String = "rust-unit-tests"

fn rust_unit_tests_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "v1-compiler unit tests" },
    id: Present { value: "rust_unit_tests" },
    run: concat(repo_self_test_command(), "\n"),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn rust_unit_tests_bound_steps() -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  [
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_checkout_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Checkout",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: toolchain_home_isolation_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Isolate toolchain homes",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_toolchain_step(),
      role: provides_only(capabilities: [
        CargoCapability, RustcCapability, RustfmtCapability, RustupCapability,
      ]),
      step_name: "Install Rust toolchain",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: toolchain_pin_rustup_default_step(),
      role: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustupCapability, CargoCapability]),
      step_name: "Pin rustup default (isolated RUSTUP_HOME has no default toolchain)",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: rust_unit_tests_step(),
      role: consumes_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability]),
      step_name: "v1-compiler unit tests",
    },
  ]
}

fn rust_unit_tests_capability_closure_holds() -> Bool {
  capability_closure_is_closed(
    v: workflow_job_capability_closure(
      steps: list_map(
        xs: rust_unit_tests_bound_steps(),
        f: fn(b) { CapabilityAnnotatedStep { step_name: b.step_name, role: b.role } },
      )
    ),
  )
}

fn rust_unit_tests_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: rust_unit_tests_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: list_map(xs: rust_unit_tests_bound_steps(), f: fn(b) { b.step }),
    needs: [],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: not_on_wet_cadence_condition() },
    timeout_minutes: 60,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE WET RECEIPTS LANE (parent ruling 2026-08-30, FLOOR-ROUTE-GAP-SELF-HOST). A separate,
// NON-REQUIRED job that executes the `v2.workflow.floor_wet_route` roster with real effects —
// a real gunbc emit, real cargo builds of the seed-linked shims, real driver runs — and lands
// the per-identity receipt the required floor's DeclinedRoutedToWetLane join refuses without.
// It runs on a daily schedule and on manual dispatch, never per pull request and never as a
// required context: one seed-linked behavioral receipt alone measured ~305s of witness wall
// (gunbc#9371), which the 2026-08-29 bankruptcy ruling prices off the required path.
//
// THE POPULATION IS DERIVED, NEVER SPELLED HERE: `claim_batch --wet-route` reads
// `floor_wet_route_roster` from the .dag authority at run time, so a roster edit needs no
// workflow regeneration and no second list can drift. The receipt — a latest-attempt JSON
// envelope keyed by the wet subject digest, plus its canonical TSV projection (authority
// `v2.workflow.floor_wet_route`) — reaches main by PULL REQUEST (transport a' as amended,
// same ruling): this job commits the refreshed pair to a well-known branch and
// opens/refreshes the PR; merging stays under the ordinary rule. No bot push to the
// protected default branch, and no API read anywhere on the required path — the floor only
// ever reads the committed pair. A RECEIPT MERGE CANNOT RELAUNCH THIS LANE by construction
// rather than by path filter: the job's condition admits only schedule and
// workflow_dispatch, and a merge to main is a push event, so the amendment's
// no-relaunch requirement holds without a paths: clause to drift.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// THE REQUIRED LANES DO NOT RUN ON THE WET CADENCE. The schedule trigger exists for the
// wet-receipts job alone; without this condition every daily tick would also pay a full
// required floor, a whole-workspace build and the unit-test job on an unchanged main — cost
// with no new subject, against the 2026-08-29 bankruptcy's whole point. Skipped-on-schedule is
// safe for the required context because a schedule run guards no merge: required checks bind
// to pull-request and merge-group heads, which the schedule event never carries.
fn not_on_wet_cadence_condition() -> String {
  "github.event_name != 'schedule'"
}

data wet_receipts_job_id: String = "wet-receipts"

// Daily, at a fixed off-peak minute; the floor's freshness budget
// (`floor_wet_route_cadence_secs` + `floor_wet_route_receipt_grace_secs`) is declared beside
// this cadence's model in `v2.workflow.floor_wet_route`, which is the authority a reader
// should price the tolerated miss count from.
data wet_receipts_cron: CronSchedule = CronSchedule {
  minute: extdeps.cron.schedule_model.Exact { value: 17 },
  hour: extdeps.cron.schedule_model.Exact { value: 9 },
  day_of_month: Wildcard,
  month: Wildcard,
  day_of_week: Wildcard
}

fn wet_receipts_job_condition() -> String {
  "github.event_name == 'schedule' || github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch'"
}

// The lane builds exactly the three bins its rows execute through: claim_batch (the wet
// executor), gunbc (the emit the cssl harness dispatches), cssl_assemble (the closure
// assembler those transports invoke).
data wet_receipts_required_bins: List<String> = ["claim_batch", "gunbc", "cssl_assemble"]

fn wet_receipts_run_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  claim_batch_command(
    mode_flag: "--wet-route",
    operands: append(
      fold(
        witness_layer_roots,
        init: [],
        f: fn(acc, root) { append(acc, items: ["--source-root", root]) },
      ),
      items: [
        "--receipt-out", floor_wet_route_receipt_json_rel_path,
        "--receipt-tsv-out", floor_wet_route_receipt_tsv_rel_path,
      ],
    ),
  )
}

fn wet_receipts_run_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Wet receipts lane (rows named by the run, not by this label)" },
    id: Present { value: "wet_receipts_run" },
    run: required_lane_run_script(command: wet_receipts_run_command()),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// THE RECEIPT LANDS EVEN WHEN THE LANE IS RED — `!cancelled()` rather than the implicit
// success() — because an honest fail receipt is the routed witnesses' verdict channel and an
// absent receipt refuses every open pull request three days later. The lane's own red stays
// on this job.
data wet_receipts_pr_annotation: String = "# dissolve-on: wet_receipts_pr_script -- orch-emitted foreign-executor step committing the lane's generated receipt pair to its refresh branch and opening the carrying pull request. Leaf git/gh strings remain until a typed repository-write effect lands on host_effect_apply (shell-to-intent Phase 2), the same terminal construction the toolchain isolation steps above wait on; the receipt pair itself is the modeled artifact (v2.workflow.floor_wet_route: JSON envelope authority, TSV canonical projection, floor-refused on drift).\n"

data wet_receipts_pr_body: String = "cd \"$ROOT\"\ngit config user.name gunbc-ci-auto-heal\ngit config user.email gunbc-ci-auto-heal@users.noreply.github.com\ngit checkout -B 'wet-receipts/refresh'\ngit add dag/gunbc/witness/wet_lane\ngit commit -m \"wet-receipts: refresh lane receipt envelope (run $\{GITHUB_RUN_ID\})\"\ngit push -f origin 'wet-receipts/refresh'\ngh pr create --head 'wet-receipts/refresh' --title 'wet-receipts: refresh lane receipt envelope' --body \"Generated artifact pair: the wet-lane latest-attempt receipt envelope and its canonical TSV projection (v2.workflow.floor_wet_route), produced by workflow run $\{GITHUB_RUN_ID\} on $\{GITHUB_SHA\}. The required floor re-projects the TSV from the JSON and refuses on drift; regenerate via the wet-receipts job, never by hand.\" || echo 'PR already open; the push refreshed it'\n"

fn wet_receipts_pr_script() -> String {
  concat(
    wet_receipts_pr_annotation,
    concat(
      "set -eu\n",
      concat("ROOT=", concat(ci_repo_root_shell(), concat("\n", wet_receipts_pr_body)))
    )
  )
}

// THE ARTIFACT UPLOAD RUNS ON EVERY EVENT AND THE COMMIT ONLY ON MAIN. A workflow_dispatch on
// a branch is how a receipt is seeded or refreshed for an in-flight change (the changed-witness
// remedy, and how this lane's own first receipt was produced): the dispatching author downloads
// the artifact and commits it on their branch, so no lane run ever pushes to a ref it does not
// own. The BuildBuddy remote executors cannot produce this receipt at all — the first attempt
// was OOM-killed at a ~0.9 GiB availability floor against the ~5 GiB the wet chain resides at —
// which is why the lane is pinned to the repository's own runners and no local fallback is
// documented.
fn wet_receipts_artifact_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Upload the receipt artifact" },
    id: none,
    uses: upload_artifact_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "name", value: yaml_string(s: "wet-lane-receipt")),
        kv(key: "path", value: yaml_string(s: "dag/gunbc/witness/wet_lane")),
        kv(key: "if-no-files-found", value: yaml_string(s: "error")),
        kv(key: "retention-days", value: yaml_int(n: 14)),
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "!cancelled() && steps.wet_receipts_run.outcome != 'skipped'" },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn wet_receipts_pr_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Commit the receipt and open its pull request" },
    id: none,
    run: wet_receipts_pr_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: Present { value: [kv(key: "GH_TOKEN", value: yaml_string(s: "${{ github.token }}"))] },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "!cancelled() && steps.wet_receipts_run.outcome != 'skipped' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'" },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn wet_receipts_bound_steps() -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  append(
    prepared_bound_steps(build_script: repo_self_build_command(bins: wet_receipts_required_bins)),
    items: [
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: wet_receipts_run_step(),
        role: consumes_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability, RustcCapability]),
        step_name: "Wet receipts lane (rows named by the run, not by this label)",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: wet_receipts_artifact_step(),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the receipt artifact",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: wet_receipts_pr_step(),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Commit the receipt and open its pull request",
      },
    ],
  )
}

fn wet_receipts_capability_closure_holds() -> Bool {
  capability_closure_is_closed(
    v: workflow_job_capability_closure(
      steps: list_map(
        xs: wet_receipts_bound_steps(),
        f: fn(b) { CapabilityAnnotatedStep { step_name: b.step_name, role: b.role } },
      )
    ),
  )
}

fn wet_receipts_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: wet_receipts_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: list_map(xs: wet_receipts_bound_steps(), f: fn(b) { b.step }),
    needs: [],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: wet_receipts_job_condition() },
    timeout_minutes: 180,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: Present {
      value: WorkflowPermissions {
        contents: Present { value: PermWrite },
        pull_requests: Present { value: PermWrite },
        issues: none,
        actions: none,
        id_token: none
      }
    }
  }
}

fn build_lane_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: build_lane_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: build_lane_steps(),
    needs: [],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: not_on_wet_cadence_condition() },
    timeout_minutes: 90,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

// THE ONE JOB THAT GATES, AND THE ONLY PLACE IN THIS FILE THAT AUTHORS SHELL TEXT.
//
// Every other step in this workflow renders a modeled value -- an ArgvCommand through
// `shell_command_render`, or `repo_self_build_command` -- and this one cannot, because there is
// no modeled value for it to render: GitHub has no declarative "this job fails unless those
// jobs succeeded". The nearest declarative form, a job-level `if` on the two results, is
// exactly the fail-open being closed -- a job whose `if` is false is SKIPPED, and a skipped
// required check does not stop a merge. So the refusal has to be a step that exits nonzero, and
// a step that exits nonzero is shell.
//
// IT IS BUILT FROM THE JOB IDS RATHER THAN SPELLING THEM. A hand-typed `needs.build.result`
// would be a second spelling of a name this file already declares, free to drift the moment
// either job is renamed -- and it would drift SILENTLY, because an unknown `needs` context
// renders as the empty string, which is not `success`, so the gate would refuse every run and
// read as a broken lane rather than as a broken reference.
//
// THE JOB-LEVEL CONDITION IS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE, AND THE FIRST CUT OF THE AGGREGATOR PUT IT
// ONLY ON THE STEP. Found in review of gunbc#9203 (review 55795), and it is the same fail-open
// this job exists to close, committed one level in -- which is exactly the failure mode DESIGN
// names as total at the level examined and blind one level down.
//
// `needs` carries an IMPLICIT JOB-LEVEL CONDITION: a job that declares `needs` and no `if` is
// SKIPPED when any needed job fails, is skipped, or is cancelled. A skipped job never starts,
// so it never reaches its steps, so a step-level `always()` cannot rescue it -- and a SKIPPED
// required check does not stop a merge. The aggregator would therefore have gone skipped-and-
// mergeable over precisely the failed lane it was introduced to catch.
//
// So the job declares a condition, which displaces that implicit default the same way a status
// function displaces a step's implicit `success()` (the substitution rule the floor's
// precondition paragraph records, and the reason a bare `needs.*.result` comparison would not
// have worked here either).
//
// IT IS `always()` AND NOT `!cancelled()`, WHICH IS THE ONE PLACE IN THIS FILE THAT DEPARTS
// FROM THE HOUSE GUARD -- stated explicitly, because a reader who knows the convention will
// otherwise correct it back. Every other guard here decides whether a STEP should run inside a
// job that is already running, and there `!cancelled()` is right. This condition decides
// something else -- whether the REQUIRED CONTEXT EXISTS AT ALL -- and under `!cancelled()` a
// cancelled run leaves the aggregator skipped, so the context reports as skipped rather than as
// a verdict.
//
// THE REASON IT IS `always()` IS NOT THE REASON IT USED TO BE, AND THE CHANGE IS THE WHOLE POINT
// OF `gunbc.floor_attempt_standing`. The argument this paragraph used to carry was a choice
// between two bad readings of cancellation -- `always()` treats every cancelled lane as a lane
// that failed, `!cancelled()` treats every cancelled workflow as a lawful supersession -- and it
// picked `always()` on the ground that noise on an abandoned head beats silence on a live one.
// That trade was real, and it was forced only because ONE CONDITION WAS BEING ASKED TO CARRY TWO
// QUESTIONS.
//
// It is not forced any more. Head-standing and lane-terminality are two independent axes, and
// the job condition owns NEITHER of them: it owns only whether this job runs. `always()` is the
// right answer to THAT question for a reason that has nothing to do with cancellation semantics
// -- a required context that can be SKIPPED turns mergeability on an unmeasured third-party
// behaviour, and under `always()` skipped disappears from the context entirely. Construction
// over validation (DESIGN 5): the ambiguous state becomes unrepresentable rather than tested.
//
// WHAT ANSWERS THE CANCELLATION QUESTION IS NOW THE GATE'S OWN BODY, where both axes are visible
// at once. `cancelled` with a measured head that is no longer the pull request's head is a
// SUPERSEDED ATTEMPT and does not block; `cancelled` with a measured head that IS still the
// pull request's head is `stands-unestablished` -- the evidence is missing on a live merge
// candidate -- and blocks, with an annotation saying it is an infrastructure event rather than a
// subject defect. Neither `cancelled -> benign` nor `cancelled -> failed` is asserted as a
// universal reading, which is exactly what both previous shapes did.
//
// WHAT IS NOT YET TRUE OF THAT, AND IT IS THE HALF A READER MUST NOT ASSUME: the first arm is
// not reachable in production today, because nothing in this workflow acquires the pull request's
// CURRENT head. The gate therefore renders head-standing as `unobserved` on every pull-request
// run and takes the fail-closed arm. The BLOCKING PREDICATE is therefore unchanged -- block iff
// either lane is not `success`, exactly as before -- while the annotation and the receipt say
// considerably more. Which runs block is identical; what they print is not.
// What changed today is the SPLIT BETWEEN RED AND UNESTABLISHED, which needs no acquisition, and
// the attempt subject printed on every run, which makes the head join computable offline by
// anyone who can already read the API. `gunbc.floor_attempt_standing`
// `floor_attempt_standing_acquisition_trigger` carries what closes the rest and what goes red
// when it does.
//
// THE COST OF `always()` IS UNCHANGED AND IS STILL PAID: a lawfully superseded run reports this
// context red, because a cancelled lane is not `success`. Measured on run 32883390033, where a
// fleet event ended both lanes and this job duly published `failure`. That red now says
// `verdict=stands-unestablished`, carries mechanism and attribution, and names the measured head
// it belongs to, which is strictly more than it said before -- but it is still a red on an
// abandoned head, and only the head acquisition removes it.
//
// WHY THAT COST IS ACCEPTED RATHER THAN TRADED AWAY, AND IT IS THE ASYMMETRY OF WHICH HEAD PAYS.
// This argument is carried over from the interim's own authority (gunbc#9302, closed in favour of
// this construction) because it survives the change of mechanism intact and is the deciding fact
// rather than a preference. Checks are tracked per head SHA, so a superseded run's red lands on a
// head that BY CONSTRUCTION will never be merged -- the push that superseded it created the head
// that will, and that head has its own run. `always()` is therefore NOISY ON ABANDONED HEADS. The
// alternative moves the cost onto the LIVE one: a lane killed with no replacement run coming --
// observed twice on 2026-08-25, once with `runner_name` empty and zero steps, once with both lanes
// ended at 18:43 and no push involved -- leaves this context SKIPPED on the head that is still the
// merge candidate.
//
// NOISE IS A COST; FAIL-OPEN IS A DEFECT (operator ruling relayed by smart-ram-730, 2026-08-26,
// deciding between the two shapes). A loud wrong-looking red gets investigated and corrected. A
// silent skip on an unverified live head does not, and it is the one failure mode nobody sees.
//
// AND THE NOISE IS MATERIALLY REDUCED WITHOUT THE ACQUISITION, which is what makes this a bearable
// trade rather than a pious one. The harm the interim was chartered against was people
// investigating FALSE SUBJECT FAILURES, and a run reporting `stands-unestablished` with a
// mechanism and an attribution does not send anyone into a diff. What is left is a red on a head
// nobody will merge, which is the cheap half of the original complaint.

// RETIRING gunbc#9339's CANCELLED-LANE EXCLUSION, AND SAYING SO RATHER THAN DELETING IT QUIETLY.
//
// That change landed while this one was in review and added
// `&& needs.build.result != 'cancelled' && needs.floor.result != 'cancelled'` here, under a
// declared DESIGN 4b(3) rung drop, carried on a `required_lanes_aggregate_cancellation_rung_drop`
// row that this change deletes along with the exclusion it declared. It was the
// right interim: with only two lane-result words in hand it could not separate the modal lawful
// supersession from an abandoned live attempt, so it removed the false alert at the only
// distinction available and DECLARED the resulting fail-open on `CurrentHeadUnestablished`
// instead of hiding it.
//
// ITS OWN RESTORATION TRIGGER NAMES THIS CONSTRUCTION BY NAME -- "the operator-ranked
// floor-current-head-binding: FloorAttemptSubject carries pull_request, measured_head, run_id;
// FloorHeadStanding is Current, Superseded or Unobservable independently of FloorLaneTerminal".
// So the exclusion and its declaration are removed here as a dissolution on climb (DESIGN 4b(4)),
// with the production machinery deleted and the evidence kept: the consolidation witnesses that
// pinned the exclusion are re-pointed at `always()` in the same change rather than dropped.
//
// WHAT IS HONESTLY RESTORED AND WHAT IS NOT, because taking the trigger's name is not the same as
// discharging it. RESTORED: the fail-open. A cancelled lane on a live head reaches this gate
// again and blocks as `stands-unestablished`, which is the half the interim declared it was
// giving up. NOT DISCHARGED: the supersession OBSERVATION. Nothing here acquires the pull
// request's current head, so a lawfully superseded run pays a red exactly as it did before the
// interim -- the noise the interim was chartered to remove comes back. That is the operator's
// ruling, not this file's choice: NOISE IS A COST, FAIL-OPEN IS A DEFECT. The remaining half of
// #9339's trigger survives unretired as
// `gunbc.floor_attempt_standing` `floor_attempt_standing_acquisition_trigger`, which is the same
// obligation under the carrier that can actually hold it.
//
// WHY THE EXCLUSION IS NOT MERELY WEAKER BUT ACTIVELY WRONG ON TODAY'S EVIDENCE, measured on this
// PR's own branch after #9339 landed: cancellation here is a BACKGROUND RATE, not a supersession
// signal. Twelve cancelled jobs across 45 minutes of one afternoon, elapsed 7s to 3062s,
// uncorrelated with duration and with any instant; seven floor jobs ran LONGER than the cancelled
// ones and passed. Two of those cancellations were live heads with no replacement run -- exactly
// the cell #9339 declared it was leaving uncovered -- and under its exclusion both would have left
// the required context skipped on a head that was still the merge candidate. The exclusion treats
// `cancelled` as evidence of supersession; the measurement says it is evidence of nothing.
fn required_lanes_aggregate_job_condition() -> String {
  concat("always() && ", not_on_wet_cadence_condition())
}
//
// THE STEP-LEVEL `always()` STAYS, and it guards a different thing rather than restating this
// one. It is about EARLIER STEPS IN THIS JOB: there are none today, so the implicit `success()`
// would be true anyway, but a step added ahead of it later would silently acquire the power to
// skip the refusal. Two conditions, two failure modes, neither a second spelling of the other.
// THE EXPRESSIONS ARE BUILT FROM THE MODELED ACTIONS EXPRESSION GRAMMAR, NOT SPELLED AS TEXT.
//
// This function nested `concat` over `"${{ needs."` and `".result }}"`, which is a second
// spelling of a language `extdeps.github.expressions` already models -- the same medium-as-string
// violation that moved the gate's shell into `gunbc.required_lanes_gate`, surviving one layer up
// in the expressions the shell interpolates. `interpolate` over a `ContextAccess` renders the
// identical text through the authority that owns the spelling, so the interpolation delimiters,
// the path separator and the context names have one home.
fn required_lane_result_expression(job_id: String) -> String {
  interpolate(expression: ContextAccess { context: Needs, path: [job_id, "result"] })
}

// THE ATTEMPT SUBJECT'S EXPRESSIONS. `github.event.pull_request.*` renders as the empty string on
// push, merge_group and workflow_dispatch runs, and that emptiness is what the gate reads to
// decide the head axis is inapplicable rather than unmeasured.
fn required_lanes_pull_request_expression() -> String {
  interpolate(expression: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["event", "pull_request", "number"] })
}

fn required_lanes_measured_head_expression() -> String {
  interpolate(
    expression: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["event", "pull_request", "head", "sha"] }
  )
}

fn required_lanes_event_name_expression() -> String {
  interpolate(expression: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["event_name"] })
}

fn required_lanes_run_id_expression() -> String {
  interpolate(expression: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["run_id"] })
}

// THE COMPARISON IS `!= "success"` AND NOT A LIST OF BAD STATES, and that is now a measured
// fact rather than a preference. Run 32883390033 (2026-08-25) had both lanes ended by a fleet
// event with no push involved, and the aggregator read:
//
//     BUILD="cancelled"   FLOOR="abandoned"
//
// `abandoned` is a `needs.*.result` value nobody here knew was reachable. An enumerated guard
// -- `== "failure" || == "cancelled"`, which is the form a reader's instinct reaches for -- would
// have admitted it and reported the required context GREEN over two lanes that never ran. The
// strict inequality admits exactly one state and refuses every other, including the ones the
// author has never heard of, which is the whole difference between a closed vocabulary and a
// remembered one (DESIGN §5).
//
// The same run is this gate's first executing receipt: the aggregator ran under `always()` with
// both lanes non-success, refused, and published `witnesses` as a FAILURE, which is the
// behaviour the job exists for and the behaviour the pre-repair shape could not produce.
// THE SHELL IS BUILT AS NODES AND SERIALIZED THROUGH THE GRAMMAR, never spelled as text.
// The first cut of this function nested `concat` over string literals and handed the result to
// a RunStep -- the medium-as-string violation DESIGN 2/3 names, raised in review 55836. Node
// construction lives in `gunbc.required_lanes_gate`; this function is the realization edge,
// following the `tools.build_step` -> `v2.workflow.bash_command_fold_serialize` precedent
// already in tree.
//
// THE REJECTED ARM REFUSES RATHER THAN RENDERING NOTHING, AND IT IS NOW BUILT FROM THE SAME
// GRAMMAR AS THE GATE. It was a raw shell String here -- an executable program spelled as text,
// which is the medium-as-string violation this PR otherwise dissolves, surviving in the one arm
// nobody reads (review 55923). The refusal program is now Nodes, in
// `gunbc.required_lanes_gate` beside the gate it stands in for, and it is serialized through the
// same fold.
//
// THE OUTCOME IS THREE-STATE, NOT TWO, and the third state is what removes the last fabricated
// value: gate serializes -> the gate; gate rejects but the refusal serializes -> the refusal,
// which stops every run loudly; both reject -> ABSENT, and `expected_witness_floor_yml` refuses
// to emit any yaml at all. A `Rejected` fold can therefore never reach a published step.
data required_lanes_gate_build_var: String = "BUILD"
data required_lanes_gate_floor_var: String = "FLOOR"
data required_lanes_gate_pull_request_var: String = "PULL_REQUEST"
data required_lanes_gate_event_name_var: String = "EVENT_NAME"
data required_lanes_gate_measured_head_var: String = "MEASURED_HEAD"
data required_lanes_gate_run_id_var: String = "RUN_ID"
data required_lanes_gate_standing_var: String = "HEAD_STANDING"
data required_lanes_gate_verdict_var: String = "VERDICT"
data required_lanes_gate_mechanism_var: String = "MECHANISM"
data required_lanes_gate_attribution_var: String = "ATTRIBUTION"

fn required_lanes_aggregate_script_outcome() -> String? {
  match bash_fold_serialize_program(
    stmts: required_lanes_gate_stmts(
      build_var: required_lanes_gate_build_var,
      floor_var: required_lanes_gate_floor_var,
      pull_request_var: required_lanes_gate_pull_request_var,
      event_name_var: required_lanes_gate_event_name_var,
      measured_head_var: required_lanes_gate_measured_head_var,
      run_id_var: required_lanes_gate_run_id_var,
      standing_var: required_lanes_gate_standing_var,
      verdict_var: required_lanes_gate_verdict_var,
      mechanism_var: required_lanes_gate_mechanism_var,
      attribution_var: required_lanes_gate_attribution_var
    ),
    set_e: false
  ) {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } => Present { value: script }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => match bash_fold_serialize_program(
      stmts: required_lanes_gate_unrenderable_stmts(),
      set_e: false
    ) {
      Accepted { value: refusal, diagnostics: _ } => Present { value: refusal }
      Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => none
    }
  }
}

fn required_lanes_gate_is_renderable() -> Bool {
  match required_lanes_aggregate_script_outcome() {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  }
}

// THE EMPTY STRING HERE IS NOT A PROGRAM AND IS NOT EMITTED. It is reachable only when
// `required_lanes_gate_is_renderable` is false, and that conjunct makes
// `expected_witness_floor_yml` return `WitnessFloorGenerationRefused`, so no yaml carrying it
// can exist. Daglang is total, so SOME value must inhabit the arm; what matters is that the
// value is not a second spelling of a shell program and that no emission path reaches it.
fn required_lanes_aggregate_script() -> String {
  match required_lanes_aggregate_script_outcome() {
    Present { value: script } => script
    Absent => ""
  }
}

// THE SUBJECT VALUES ARE BOUND AS ENVIRONMENT, WHICH IS THE ONLY PLACE AN EXPRESSION AND A
// SHELL PROGRAM MAY MEET.
//
// A `${{ }}` expression is substituted into the workflow document before any shell exists, so
// it has to reach the emitted yaml verbatim. A yaml scalar does that -- `emit_inline_scalar_text`
// renders an expression unquoted and unescaped -- while a bash literal word cannot, because
// quoting it is exactly what the bash serializer is for. `gunbc.required_lanes_gate` therefore
// takes variable NAMES only and reads them as shell variables; the expressions live here, one
// row per variable, joined to the gate by the same declarations the gate is handed.
fn required_lanes_aggregate_step_env() -> List<YamlKeyValue> {
  [
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_build_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lane_result_expression(job_id: build_lane_job_id))),
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_floor_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lane_result_expression(job_id: floor_lane_job_id))),
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_pull_request_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lanes_pull_request_expression())),
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_event_name_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lanes_event_name_expression())),
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_measured_head_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lanes_measured_head_expression())),
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_run_id_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lanes_run_id_expression()))
  ]
}

fn required_lanes_aggregate_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Both required lanes must have succeeded" },
    id: none,
    run: required_lanes_aggregate_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: Present { value: required_lanes_aggregate_step_env() },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "always()" },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// It checks out nothing, installs nothing and builds nothing: it reads two results. A five
// minute bound is an order of magnitude above anything that can take, and it stops a stuck
// aggregator from holding a runner for the inherited six hours.
fn required_lanes_aggregate_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: witness_floor_workflow_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: [required_lanes_aggregate_step()],
    needs: [build_lane_job_id, floor_lane_job_id],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: required_lanes_aggregate_job_condition() },
    timeout_minutes: 5,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

data witness_floor_workflow: Workflow = {
  name: witness_floor_workflow_name,
  on: [
    WorkflowDispatch { inputs: [] },
    Schedule { cron: wet_receipts_cron },
    MergeGroup,
    Push { branches: [gunbc_default_branch_name], paths: [] },
    PullRequest {
      branches: [gunbc_default_branch_name],
      types: [Opened, Synchronize, Reopened]
    }
  ],
  concurrency: Present {
    value: ConcurrencyMappingQueueNotMax {
      group: witness_floor_concurrency_group,
      cancel_in_progress: Present {
        value: CancelInProgressExpression {
          expression: witness_floor_cancel_in_progress_expression
        }
      },
      explicit_single: none
    }
  },
  env: Present {
    value: [
      kv(key: "CARGO_TERM_COLOR", value: yaml_string(s: "always")),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_EXPECTED_RED_ROSTER_JOIN", value: yaml_string(s: expected_red_roster_join_path)),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_REQUIRED_FLOOR_DISPOSITION", value: yaml_string(s: required_floor_disposition_path)),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_LONG_HOME_STORAGE_AGREEMENT", value: yaml_string(s: long_home_storage_agreement_path)),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_REQUIRED_FLOOR_CLAIM_COST", value: yaml_string(s: required_floor_claim_cost_path))
    ]
  },
  permissions: Present {
    value: WorkflowPermissions {
      contents: Present { value: PermRead },
      pull_requests: none,
      issues: none,
      actions: none,
      id_token: none
    }
  },
  jobs: [build_lane_job(), witness_floor_job(), rust_unit_tests_job(), wet_receipts_job(), required_lanes_aggregate_job()]
}

// THE CLOSURE HAS A PRODUCTION CONSUMER, which is the difference between a check and a claim.
// A capability check that only tests call is specification-without-execution: it can be green
// forever while the emitter happily produces the broken workflow beside it (DESIGN §5).
//
// So emission refuses. A job whose cargo consumer has no provider ordered before it yields the
// refusal marker instead of yaml -- not a warning, and not yaml-with-a-comment, because the
// generated-artifact drift gate compares bytes and a refusal marker cannot be mistaken for a
// workflow. This is the deploy.yml failure made unemittable rather than merely detectable: that
// workflow ran `cargo build` as step two with no toolchain provider anywhere in the file, and
// nothing in this repository could refuse it.
data witness_floor_capability_closure_refusal: String = "REFUSED gunbc.witness_floor_workflow: a step consumes a capability that no preceding step provides (gunbc.workflow_capability_closure). No workflow yaml was emitted. Fix the step roles in witness_floor_bound_steps or build_lane_bound_steps, or the order of the steps themselves."

// THE CLOSURE IS CHECKED PER JOB, AND BOTH JOBS ARE CHECKED. A capability is provided by a step
// INSIDE a job -- the toolchain install does not reach across a job boundary -- so a
// whole-workflow closure would be the wrong shape, and checking only the job that existed first
// would leave the new one guarded by nothing while the emission still refused on its behalf.
// That is the inert half DESIGN §6 names: machinery that exists and gates nothing.
data witness_floor_capability_closure_per_job_note: String = "expected_witness_floor_yml conjoins required_lanes_gate_is_renderable and witness_floor_capability_closure_holds and build_lane_capability_closure_holds and rust_unit_tests_capability_closure_holds and wet_receipts_capability_closure_holds; adding another job without adding its conjunct would emit that job unchecked."

type WitnessFloorGenerationOutcome
  = WitnessFloorGenerated { content: String }
  | WitnessFloorGenerationRefused { reason: String }

// THE REFUSAL NOW CARRIES ITS CAUSE. It did not before: gunbc.generated_artifact_emit named one
// static string for every arm, so a toolchain-home refusal and a capability-closure refusal reached
// the operator as the same sentence. The two have nothing in common but the file they suppress.
data witness_floor_capability_closure_refusal_reason: String = "REFUSED gunbc.witness_floor_workflow: a lane's capability closure does not hold, or the required-lanes gate is not renderable. No workflow yaml was emitted. Fix the step roles in the lane's annotations, or the order of the steps themselves."

fn expected_witness_floor_yml() -> WitnessFloorGenerationOutcome {
  match admit_workflow_toolchain_homes(workflow: witness_floor_workflow) {
    WorkflowToolchainHomeRefused { job_id: j, refusal: r } =>
      WitnessFloorGenerationRefused { reason: toolchain_home_refusal_reason(job_id: j, refusal: r) }
    WorkflowToolchainHomesAdmitted =>
      if witness_floor_capability_closure_holds() && build_lane_capability_closure_holds() && rust_unit_tests_capability_closure_holds() && wet_receipts_capability_closure_holds() && required_lanes_gate_is_renderable() {
        WitnessFloorGenerated { content: serialize_yaml(v: project_workflow_to_yaml(workflow: witness_floor_workflow)) }
      } else {
        WitnessFloorGenerationRefused { reason: witness_floor_capability_closure_refusal_reason }
      }
  }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// FIRST-EXECUTION FINDINGS (2026-08-15). Three rulings, recorded here beside the emission they
// are about rather than in a plan document that would drift from it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

data witness_floor_first_execution_receipt: String = "THE FOLD'S FIRST REAL EXECUTION REFUSED, AND THAT IS THE FINDING. Run on GitHub (12m24s whole job) and reproduced locally (531s for the fold alone): strict preparation refused with one typed located diagnostic and ZERO witnesses executed. Measured cost shape inside the fold — frontend 38s, normalize 2s, reconcile ~8 minutes, analyses 1s; reconcile IS the fold. Diagnostic census after repairing this cut's own residue: 282 diagnostics across 75 files (57 test/fixture, 18 production). CORRECTION 2026-08-15: this sentence first read that ZERO of them referenced anything this cut deleted. That was FALSE, and the method that produced it is the error worth keeping. The check was a grep of the refusal set for `ci_floor|falsifier|selection|scoped_witness|pr_native|fast_lane` — a list of names I expected — so it could only ever find residue I had already thought of. Two carriers declared in the deleted `affected_set_floor_runner` (`FloorDiffLineTouch`, `DiffPathsProduced`) matched none of those patterns and sat in the census unattributed, along with the two witness files that consumed them. The sound check, run afterwards, asks the question from the other side: for every unresolved symbol in the refusal set, is it declared anywhere in the live tree, and if not, was it declared in a file this branch deleted. That found them immediately. A grep for names you predict is not a census; it is a confirmation of your own list, and this is the second time in one day this lane produced an incomplete enumeration while documenting the incomplete-enumeration class. Against the old floor's 56.5 minutes this is 8m51s, but the comparison is NOT like-for-like and must not be quoted as one: a refusing run that executed nothing is cheaper than a run that executed 9,000 witnesses for reasons that have nothing to do with the fold's design."

data witness_floor_whole_tree_strict_concealment_note: String = "WHAT THE REFUSAL ACTUALLY DISCOVERED: this repository has never been whole-tree strict-typechecked. The old floor prepared PER-ENTRY closures, so a broken module reddened only the closures that imported it and was invisible everywhere else; a module nothing imported was never typechecked at all. This fold's admission wall is the first mechanism that has ever demanded the whole tree resolve as one namespace, and it cannot yet. That is a pre-existing concealment surfaced, not a regression introduced — and it is a larger finding than the fold's wall-clock. Note the same asymmetry inside this lane's own verification: `gunbc compile --target dag` resolves a transitive import closure and reports the remaining 1,135 modules as census-only, which is why this cut's own six orphaned importers passed a whole-corpus compile and were caught only by the fold."

data witness_floor_all_or_nothing_upheld_note: String = "ALL-OR-NOTHING PREPARATION IS UPHELD (ruling 2026-08-15), and the reasoning is recorded because the temptation to relax it recurs. The subject IS the corpus. Admitting a partially-typechecking subject while reporting a whole-corpus verdict is the empty-observation narrow — rendering 'I could not admit this population' as 'this population is fine' — which DESIGN names as strictly worse than the widen section 5 already forbids, because a widen is merely expensive while a narrow is silently uncovered. The ordering consequence is real and is owned by the operator rather than by this lane: the fold's first green day requires the last pre-existing defect to be fixed."

data witness_floor_bare_cross_module_reference_class_note: String = "THE LARGEST DIAGNOSTIC CLASS IS NOT THIS LANE'S TO FIX, and it must not be fixed the obvious way. 151 of the 185 undefined-variable diagnostics are one name: `Empty`. CORRECTED 2026-08-15, same day, against this note's own first revision: that revision said `Empty` is the nullary constructor of `FreeMonoid` in `std.algebra`, declared once. It is declared TWICE — `std.stack` `Stack` and `std.algebra` `FreeMonoid` — and the error was reproduced independently on both sides of the relay, because each of us grepped the module we had been pointed at and neither swept the tree for a second declaration of the name. That is the incomplete-enumeration class committed while diagnosing the incomplete-enumeration class, and it is recorded here rather than quietly amended because the corrected fact changes the remedy.\n\nWHAT THESE SITES ACTUALLY ARE: bare references to a TWO-CANDIDATE HOMONYM, where neither candidate sits on the referencing module's containment chain (verified on the settling specimen, `v2.lens.enforcement.cost_coverage`, which carries exactly one unrelated import and uses bare `Empty` in its body). The old per-entry path admitted them by POOL-MEMBERSHIP COINCIDENCE — some unrelated entry in that closure happened to drag the right module into the pool — which is the corpus-wide accidental-coverage trap measured in full by test.claim.import_admission_closure_membership_witness_test import_admission_closure_membership_note (four arms; this is its arm (3), pool-coincidence), over the resolution rule stated in v1.04_env global_bare_fallback_invariant. Strict whole-corpus preparation has no such coincidence to rely on.\n\nTHE REMEDY, and what it is NOT: containment-based resolution cannot bind these, because there is nothing on the chain to bind them to; the honest answer at those sites is a refusal for ambiguity. So this is corpus AUTHORING work — each site takes a qualified reference (`std.algebra.Empty`) — and NOT resolver work. The first revision of this note recorded the fold's first green as downstream of the namespace cut's resolution model; that dependency is STRUCK, because it was a promise made on the namespace lane's behalf that its own measurement showed it could not keep. DO NOT REPAIR BY ADDING IMPORT LINES: that ruling stands unchanged, and its reason is now sharper — a qualified reference IS the dependency edge, which is the end state; an import line is the grammar being deleted.\n\nNOT STARTED HERE, deliberately: 151 qualified references authored on this branch would collide with the namespace branch's rewrite of the same files, so sequencing across two live branches is an operator decision. The single dual-std `nat_compare` ambiguity (`std.nat` vs `v2.std.nat`) is the same shape and confirms the pattern is the dual std tree under one flat namespace rather than an `Empty` quirk."

data witness_floor_memory_envelope_finding: String = "THE FOLD'S MEMORY ENVELOPE IS A FIRST-CLASS PROPERTY, measured rather than estimated. One run was OOM-KILLED (exit 137 at 537s, during strict preparation — a kill, not a refusal). The next run was sampled: RSS climbed 3.9 -> 4.8 -> 5.8 -> 6.3 GiB over 90 seconds and PEAKED AT 9.37 GiB. The container cap (31 GiB) is not the binding constraint; the shared 20 GiB slice is, and the kernel kills the LARGEST task in it when the aggregate is exceeded, which is the likely cause of the kill. CONSEQUENCE, named because it is what makes this rank beside the diagnostic census: non-deterministic OOM on a shared host is exactly the shape that makes a required check flaky, and a check that dies for reasons unrelated to its subject teaches everyone to ignore it. DIRECTION RULED, MEASUREMENT NOT STARTED: do NOT re-shard into batches or worker processes to make it fit — that is the deleted decomposition rebuilding itself inside the replacement one convenience at a time, and the single prepared subject is precisely what the fold exists to be. The 9.37 GiB peak is a cost-shape defect to root-cause (the repository already carries that lane: schedule-derived per-module retention, and the carrier/interning roots), not a reason to change the shape."
